const QS = [
{id:1,q:"What are the types of jurisdiction under the CPC?",a:"The CPC recognises three types of jurisdiction. Territorial jurisdiction determines which court can try the suit based on where the cause of action arose or where the defendant resides (Sections 15-20). Pecuniary jurisdiction sets the monetary ceiling a court can adjudicate. Subject-matter jurisdiction restricts certain courts to specific categories of disputes. A decree passed without jurisdiction is a nullity.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Trilegal","Khaitan","SAM"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:2,q:"What is res judicata under Section 11 CPC? How does it differ from res sub judice (Section 10)?",a:"Res judicata (Section 11) bars re-litigation of an issue already decided between the same parties by a competent court. Res sub judice (Section 10) stays a later-filed suit where a previously instituted suit between the same parties on the same matter is still pending. Key distinction: res judicata applies after final adjudication; res sub judice applies while the earlier suit is still pending. Constructive res judicata extends the bar to issues that could and ought to have been raised but were not.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Trilegal","Khaitan"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:3,q:"When can a plaint be rejected under Order VII Rule 11?",a:"A plaint can be rejected on four grounds: (1) it does not disclose a cause of action; (2) the relief claimed is undervalued and the plaintiff fails to correct after being given time; (3) the suit is barred by any law (e.g., limitation, res judicata); (4) required copies not furnished. The court examines only the averments in the plaint at face value. Rejection under Order VII Rule 11 is not a decision on merits and the plaintiff may file a fresh suit.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Trilegal"],track:"Litigation",detailed:false},
{id:4,q:"Explain the three-part test for granting a temporary injunction under Order XXXIX.",a:"Courts apply three cumulative tests: (1) Prima facie case -- the applicant must show a fair question for trial; (2) Balance of convenience -- greater harm would fall on the applicant if refused than on the respondent if granted; (3) Irreparable injury -- damages would not be an adequate remedy. All three must be satisfied. Gujarat Bottling v. Coca Cola added that the applicant must approach court with clean hands.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","SAM"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:5,q:"Distinguish between appeal, revision, and review under the CPC.",a:"Appeal (Sections 96-100): statutory right for higher court to re-examine facts and law; first appeal on both facts and law, second appeal (Section 100) only on a substantial question of law. Revision (Section 115): supervisory -- High Court checks whether subordinate court acted within jurisdiction, not merits. Review (Section 114, Order XLVII): application to the same court to reconsider on narrow grounds -- new evidence, mistake on face of record, or sufficient reason. Appeal is widest, revision is jurisdictional, review is narrowest.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","SAM","JSA"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:6,q:"What is the difference between a decree, judgment, and order?",a:"Judgment (Section 2(9)): statement of the grounds for the decree or order. Decree (Section 2(2)): formal expression of adjudication conclusively determining rights of parties -- drawn up separately after the judgment. Order (Section 2(14)): formal expression of any decision that is not a decree. Key distinction: a decree is always appealable; an order is only appealable if specifically made so by the CPC. A decree can be preliminary or final.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","SAM","JSA"],track:"Litigation",detailed:false},
{id:7,q:"What is the procedure for summary suits under Order XXXVII?",a:"Summary suits apply to suits on bills of exchange, hundies, promissory notes, or where the plaintiff seeks only liquidated money damages based on a written contract. The defendant cannot defend as of right -- they must apply for leave to defend and show a triable defence. If the court finds no real defence, summary judgment is granted. If leave is granted, the suit proceeds as an ordinary suit.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Khaitan"],track:"Litigation",detailed:false},
{id:8,q:"What is the difference between Section 9 and Section 17 of the Arbitration Act?",a:"Section 9 grants the court power to order interim measures before, during, or after arbitral proceedings -- including securing the amount in dispute, preservation of property, and interim injunctions. Section 17 grants the arbitral tribunal the same powers once constituted. Post-2015 Amendment, Section 17 orders are enforceable as court orders. Key distinction is timing: Section 9 most relevant before the tribunal is constituted; once it exists, parties should approach the tribunal under Section 17.",topic:"Arbitration",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Trilegal","Khaitan","JSA"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:9,q:"What are the essentials of a valid arbitration agreement under Section 7?",a:"Section 7 requires: (1) agreement must be in writing; (2) must express intention of parties to submit disputes to arbitration; (3) can be an arbitration clause in a contract or a separate agreement; (4) in writing includes electronic communication. An exchange of letters where one party alleges an agreement and the other does not deny it also satisfies the requirement. The agreement must relate to a defined legal relationship and the subject matter must be arbitrable.",topic:"Arbitration",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Trilegal","Khaitan","JSA"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:10,q:"What is the doctrine of Kompetenz-Kompetenz (Section 16)?",a:"Kompetenz-Kompetenz means the arbitral tribunal has power to rule on its own jurisdiction, including objections to the existence or validity of the arbitration agreement. Under Section 16, the tribunal can decide whether a valid arbitration agreement exists, and challenging the main contract does not automatically invalidate the arbitration clause. A jurisdictional objection must be raised no later than submission of the statement of defence.",topic:"Arbitration",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Trilegal","Khaitan"],track:"Litigation",detailed:false},
{id:11,q:"What is the separability doctrine in arbitration?",a:"The separability doctrine holds that the arbitration clause is an independent agreement, separate from the underlying contract. Even if the main contract is void, voidable, illegal, or terminated, the arbitration clause survives and the tribunal can adjudicate disputes including the validity of the main contract itself. Codified in Section 16(1) of the Arbitration Act.",topic:"Arbitration",companies:["CAM","AZB","Trilegal","Khaitan"],track:"Litigation",detailed:false},
{id:12,q:"What are the grounds for setting aside an arbitral award under Section 34?",a:"Section 34 permits setting aside on: (1) party incapacity; (2) invalid arbitration agreement; (3) lack of proper notice; (4) award beyond scope of submission; (5) tribunal composition contrary to agreement. Additionally, if the subject matter is not arbitrable or the award conflicts with public policy of India. Post-2015 Amendment, public policy is confined to: fraud/corruption, contravention of fundamental policy of Indian law, or conflict with basic notions of morality or justice. Patent illegality is a ground only for domestic arbitrations.",topic:"Arbitration",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Trilegal","Khaitan","JSA"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:13,q:"What is the difference between the seat and venue of arbitration? Discuss BALCO.",a:"The seat determines the juridical home -- which country's courts have supervisory jurisdiction. The venue is merely the physical hearing location. In BALCO v. Kaiser Aluminium (2012), the Supreme Court held that Part I applies only to India-seated arbitrations; Part II covers foreign-seated award enforcement. If parties designate a foreign seat, Indian courts generally lack supervisory jurisdiction. BGS SGS Soma clarified that designating a venue with supranational institutional rules can imply the venue is the seat unless expressly separated.",topic:"Arbitration",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Trilegal","Khaitan","JSA"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:14,q:"What is an emergency arbitrator and are emergency awards enforceable in India?",a:"An emergency arbitrator is appointed by an arbitral institution before the full tribunal is constituted to grant urgent interim relief. In Amazon v. Future Retail (2021), the Supreme Court held that an emergency arbitrator's order is enforceable under Section 17(1), treating the emergency arbitrator as part of the arbitral tribunal. This was landmark -- prior to this, enforceability was uncertain.",topic:"Arbitration",companies:["CAM","AZB","Trilegal","SAM"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:15,q:"What is the difference between domestic, international commercial, and foreign-seated arbitration?",a:"Domestic arbitration: Indian parties, seated in India -- Part I applies fully. International commercial arbitration (Section 2(1)(f)): at least one foreign party -- if seated in India, Part I applies with modifications (patent illegality unavailable under Section 34). Foreign-seated arbitration: governed by Part II; Indian courts have only enforcement jurisdiction. The seat determines which Part applies.",topic:"Arbitration",companies:["CAM","AZB","Trilegal","SAM"],track:"Litigation",detailed:false},
{id:16,q:"Name prominent arbitral institutions and explain institutional vs. ad hoc arbitration.",a:"India: MCIA (Mumbai), DIAC (Delhi). International: SIAC (Singapore), LCIA (London), ICC (Paris), HKIAC (Hong Kong). Institutional arbitration provides rules, secretariat, fee schedules, and administrative support. Ad hoc arbitration is party-designed (often using UNCITRAL Rules), cheaper but relies on party cooperation. Institutional is preferred for high-value cross-border disputes.",topic:"Arbitration",companies:["CAM","AZB","Trilegal","Khaitan","JSA"],track:"Litigation",detailed:false},
{id:17,q:"What is the difference between Section 7, Section 9, and Section 10 petitions under the IBC?",a:"Section 7 (financial creditor): need only show debt, default, and complete application. Section 9 (operational creditor): must serve demand notice, show debtor's failure to pay/dispute within 10 days, and absence of pre-existing dispute (Mobilox v. Kirusa). Section 10: corporate debtor self-triggers insolvency. Threshold raised to Rs.1 crore. Operational creditors have no CoC voting rights.",topic:"IBC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Trilegal","Khaitan","JSA","SAM"],track:"Both",detailed:true},
{id:18,q:"Walk me through the CIRP under the IBC.",a:"CIRP begins on NCLT admission. IRP appointed within 14 days. Section 14 moratorium takes effect. IRP takes control, collates claims, constitutes CoC. CoC may replace IRP with RP. RP invites resolution plans from eligible applicants (Section 29A disqualifications apply). CoC votes -- 66% approval needed. NCLT sanctions the approved plan. Must conclude within 330 days. If no plan approved, company enters liquidation.",topic:"IBC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Trilegal","Khaitan","JSA","SAM"],track:"Both",detailed:true},
{id:19,q:"What is the moratorium under Section 14 IBC? Does it bar arbitration?",a:"The moratorium prohibits: institution/continuation of suits against the corporate debtor; execution against debtor assets; foreclosure/enforcement of security; recovery of debtor property; disconnection of essential services. On arbitration: the moratorium stays proceedings against the debtor but does not automatically stay all arbitral proceedings. Arbitrations by the debtor may continue; money claims against the debtor are generally stayed.",topic:"IBC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Trilegal","Khaitan","JSA"],track:"Both",detailed:true},
{id:20,q:"Explain Section 29A IBC -- who is ineligible to submit a resolution plan?",a:"Section 29A disqualifies: undischarged insolvents; wilful defaulters; NPA account holders for 1+ year who have not cleared overdues; persons convicted of offences with 2+ year punishment; disqualified directors; connected persons of the above. Targets promoters of failed companies from buying back distressed assets cheaply.",topic:"IBC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Trilegal","Khaitan","SAM"],track:"Both",detailed:true},
{id:21,q:"Explain the waterfall mechanism under Section 53 IBC.",a:"Priority order: (1) insolvency resolution/liquidation costs; (2) secured creditors (who relinquished security) and workmen's dues (24 months); (3) employee wages (12 months); (4) unsecured financial debts; (5) government dues; (6) remaining debts; (7) preference shareholders; (8) equity shareholders. This overrides other statutes giving priority to government dues. Confirmed in ESSAR Steel.",topic:"IBC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Trilegal","Khaitan","JSA"],track:"Both",detailed:true},
{id:22,q:"What is the difference between cognizable and non-cognizable offences?",a:"Cognizable: police can arrest without warrant and investigate without Magistrate's order (e.g., murder, robbery). FIR must be registered. Non-cognizable: police need Magistrate's order for investigation, cannot arrest without warrant. Classification per the First Schedule to CrPC/BNSS. Bailable/non-bailable is a separate distinction.",topic:"Criminal Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","Khaitan","JSA","Karanjawala"],track:"Litigation",detailed:false},
{id:23,q:"What is anticipatory bail? What factors do courts consider?",a:"Anticipatory bail (Section 438 CrPC) is a direction to release on bail if arrested -- granted before arrest for non-bailable offences. Sushila Aggarwal (2020): can be granted without time limits. Factors: nature/gravity of accusation, applicant's antecedents, flight risk, whether accusation is to injure/humiliate, availability for investigation. Gurbaksh Singh Sibbia laid the foundational principles.",topic:"Criminal Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","Khaitan","Karanjawala","JSA"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:24,q:"What is default bail under Section 167(2) CrPC?",a:"An indefeasible right accruing when the charge sheet is not filed within: 60 days for offences up to 10 years imprisonment, 90 days for death/life/10+ year offences. Once expired without charge sheet, the accused has a right to bail not defeated by subsequent filing. The Supreme Court held this right flows from Article 21.",topic:"Criminal Law",companies:["CAM","Khaitan","SAM","Karanjawala"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:25,q:"What is the scope of quashing under Section 482 CrPC? Discuss Bhajan Lal categories.",a:"Section 482 preserves HC's inherent powers to prevent abuse of process. Bhajan Lal (1992) categories: (1) allegations do not make out an offence; (2) absurd/improbable allegations; (3) legal bar exists; (4) abuse of process; (5) allegations insufficient even at face value; (6) no evidence supports the charge; (7) mala fide criminal proceedings. Must be exercised sparingly.",topic:"Criminal Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","Khaitan","Karanjawala","JSA"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:26,q:"What is the scheme of PMLA? What are proceeds of crime?",a:"PMLA criminalises making proceeds of crime appear legitimate. Proceeds = property derived from a scheduled offence. ED investigates via ECIR, can attach property (provisional 180 days, then confirmed), arrest under Section 19, prosecute before Special Court. Vijay Madanlal Choudhary (2022) upheld PMLA's constitutional validity including reverse burden for bail.",topic:"White Collar",companies:["Khaitan","CAM","SAM","JSA"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:27,q:"What are the twin conditions for bail under Section 45 PMLA?",a:"(1) Public Prosecutor must be heard; (2) court must be satisfied there are reasonable grounds to believe the accused is not guilty AND unlikely to commit offences while on bail. Creates a reverse burden -- accused must show reasonable grounds of innocence. Upheld in Vijay Madanlal Choudhary. Pankaj Bansal introduced arrest safeguards.",topic:"White Collar",companies:["Khaitan","CAM","SAM","JSA","Karanjawala"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:28,q:"What is a Section 138 NI Act proceeding (cheque dishonour)?",a:"Section 138 criminalises cheque dishonour for insufficient funds. Procedure: present cheque within validity, receive dishonour memo, send demand notice within 30 days, file complaint within 30 days of notice period expiry if unpaid. Compoundable at any stage. Max punishment: 2 years imprisonment and/or fine up to twice cheque amount.",topic:"Criminal Law",companies:["CAM","Khaitan","SAM","JSA","Karanjawala"],track:"Litigation",detailed:false},
{id:29,q:"What are the five types of writs?",a:"(1) Habeas Corpus -- produce the body; release from unlawful detention. (2) Mandamus -- compel a public authority to perform a mandatory duty. (3) Certiorari -- quash an order exceeding jurisdiction or violating natural justice. (4) Prohibition -- prevent a lower court from exceeding jurisdiction (prospective). (5) Quo Warranto -- challenge unauthorized holding of public office.",topic:"Constitutional Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Karanjawala","Agarwal Law Associates","Parekh & Co."],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:30,q:"What is the difference between Article 32 and Article 226?",a:"Article 32 is a fundamental right for SC enforcement of fundamental rights only -- SC cannot refuse. Article 226 empowers HC to issue writs for fundamental rights AND any other purpose (wider scope) but is discretionary -- HC may refuse citing alternative remedy. Article 32 is available only for fundamental rights; Article 226 for any legal right.",topic:"Constitutional Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Karanjawala","Agarwal Law Associates","Parekh & Co."],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:31,q:"What is the basic structure doctrine?",a:"Parliament's amendment power under Article 368 cannot alter the Constitution's basic structure. Established in Kesavananda Bharati (1973). Includes: supremacy of Constitution, republican/democratic form, secularism, separation of powers, federalism, sovereignty, individual freedom, rule of law, judicial review, fundamental rights-directive principles balance. Exact contours evolve through judicial interpretation.",topic:"Constitutional Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Karanjawala","Parekh & Co."],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:32,q:"What is a PIL? What is the test for locus standi?",a:"A PIL is filed by a public-spirited person on behalf of those who cannot approach court. Traditional locus standi is relaxed -- the petitioner need show sufficient interest and genuine public interest, not personal injury. PILs may be filed under Article 32 (SC) or Article 226 (HC). Courts impose costs on frivolous PILs.",topic:"Constitutional Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","Khaitan","Karanjawala","Agarwal Law Associates"],track:"Litigation",detailed:false},
{id:33,q:"What is the difference between admissibility and relevancy?",a:"Relevancy is wider -- a fact is relevant if it logically tends to prove or disprove a fact in issue. Admissibility is narrower -- determines whether a relevant fact can be received by the court. All admissible evidence must be relevant, but not all relevant evidence is admissible (e.g., spousal privilege). Sections 5-55 deal with relevancy; admissibility governed by Evidence Act and procedural laws.",topic:"Evidence",companies:["CAM","SAM","Khaitan","Karanjawala","Parekh & Co."],track:"Litigation",detailed:false},
{id:34,q:"How is electronic evidence proved? Discuss Section 65B.",a:"Section 65B governs admissibility of electronic records via a certificate identifying the record, describing production, and certifying proper computer operation. Arjun Panditrao Khotkar (2020): certificate is mandatory for secondary electronic evidence -- oral evidence cannot substitute. If the owner produces the original device, a certificate may not be needed (primary evidence).",topic:"Evidence",companies:["CAM","SAM","Khaitan","Karanjawala","Trilegal"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:35,q:"What is the difference between primary and secondary evidence?",a:"Primary (Section 62): original document produced in court. Secondary (Section 63): certified copies, mechanical copies, oral accounts. Secondary evidence admissible only when original cannot be produced (Section 65) -- destroyed, in adverse party's possession, or impracticable. For electronic evidence, Section 65B creates a special secondary evidence regime.",topic:"Evidence",companies:["CAM","SAM","Khaitan","Karanjawala"],track:"Litigation",detailed:false},
{id:36,q:"What are the essentials of a valid contract under the Indian Contract Act, 1872?",a:"Section 10 requires: (1) Free consent (not vitiated by coercion, undue influence, fraud, misrepresentation, mistake); (2) Competency (age of majority, sound mind, not disqualified); (3) Lawful consideration (not forbidden, fraudulent, injurious, immoral, against public policy); (4) Lawful object; (5) Not expressly declared void (restraint of trade S.27, restraint of marriage S.26).",topic:"Contract Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA","ELP","NDA"],track:"Both",detailed:true},
{id:37,q:"Distinguish between void, voidable, and unenforceable contracts.",a:"Void (Section 2(j)): no legal effect from inception, unenforceable by either party (e.g., minor's agreement, unlawful consideration). Voidable (Section 2(i)): valid until the aggrieved party avoids it -- enforceable at one party's option (e.g., contracts induced by coercion/fraud). Unenforceable: valid in substance but unenforceable due to technical defect (e.g., insufficient stamp duty).",topic:"Contract Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Both",detailed:false},
{id:38,q:"What is the doctrine of frustration under Section 56?",a:"A contract becomes void when performance becomes impossible or unlawful after formation by an event the promisor could not prevent. Indian law treats frustration as discharge by operation of law. Events: destruction of subject matter, illegality, death/incapacity, war. Mere commercial hardship is NOT frustration -- Energy Watchdog held fuel price rise did not frustrate a PPA. Must be impossible, not merely onerous.",topic:"Contract Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Both",detailed:true},
{id:39,q:"What is the difference between liquidated damages and a penalty clause?",a:"Under Section 74, India has no rigid LD/penalty distinction as in English law. The party suffering breach receives reasonable compensation not exceeding the amount named regardless of labelling. Court determines reasonableness -- can reduce if disproportionate. ONGC v. Saw Pipes: Section 74 does not require proof of actual loss if the sum is a genuine pre-estimate, but reasonableness is the overriding test.",topic:"Contract Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA","ELP"],track:"Both",detailed:true},
{id:40,q:"Distinguish between a contract of indemnity and a contract of guarantee.",a:"Indemnity (Section 124): two parties -- indemnifier promises to save indemnified from loss; liability is primary and independent. Guarantee (Section 126): three parties -- surety guarantees debtor's performance to creditor; surety's liability is secondary, contingent on debtor's default. Guarantee creates three contracts; indemnity creates one. Surety has subrogation rights after paying creditor.",topic:"Contract Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Both",detailed:true},
{id:41,q:"What is specific performance and when is it granted?",a:"Post-2018 Amendment to Specific Relief Act, specific performance is the default -- Section 10 shall be enforced subject to limited exceptions. Previously it was discretionary (only when damages inadequate). Exceptions: substituted performance available (Section 20), personal skill contracts, determinable contracts, unjust circumstances. Contracts for immovable property almost always attract specific performance.",topic:"Contract Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Both",detailed:true},
{id:42,q:"What is the difference between an SPA and a BTA (slump sale agreement)?",a:"SPA transfers shares -- buyer acquires the company as a whole with all assets, liabilities, contracts, employees. BTA transfers an identified business undertaking as a going concern for lump sum -- only specified assets/liabilities transfer; seller entity continues. SPA stamp duty is minimal (0.015% demat shares); BTA attracts conveyance duty (5-7%). BTA requires counterparty consent for restricted contracts; SPA does not change the contracting entity. Tax differs -- slump sale under Section 50B.",topic:"M&A",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:43,q:"What is due diligence? What are the key areas covered?",a:"Comprehensive investigation of the target before signing. Covers: (1) corporate matters; (2) material contracts; (3) regulatory compliance/licences; (4) litigation; (5) real estate; (6) IP; (7) employment; (8) tax; (9) FEMA/FDI compliance; (10) environmental and data privacy. Findings inform reps, warranties, and indemnities in transaction documents.",topic:"M&A",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA","NDA","ELP"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:44,q:"What are representations and warranties vs. an indemnity in an SPA?",a:"Representations: statements of existing fact to induce the contract. Warranties: contractual promises that facts are true -- breach gives damages claim. Indemnity: separate promise to hold harmless for specific losses pound-for-pound, without proving foreseeability/mitigation. Indemnities for identified risks from DD; warranties for broader protection. Distinction matters for damages measure and limitation periods.",topic:"M&A",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:45,q:"What are MAC (Material Adverse Change) clauses?",a:"MAC clauses allow the buyer to withdraw if a material adverse change occurs to the target between signing and closing. Sellers push exclusions (law changes, market conditions, pandemics); buyers want breadth. Burden of proving MAC falls on the invoking party. Indian courts would apply ICA Section 56/62 and contractual interpretation. MAC functions as a risk-allocation mechanism.",topic:"M&A",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:46,q:"Explain tag-along and drag-along rights.",a:"Tag-along (co-sale): protects minority -- if majority sells, minority can join on same terms/price. Prevents majority exiting at premium while minority is locked in. Drag-along: protects majority -- forces minority to sell to a third-party buyer on same terms, ensuring no minority block on a 100% sale. Both are contractual (SHA/SSA) and should be mirrored in AoA for enforceability.",topic:"M&A",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA","NDA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:47,q:"Why do investors prefer CCPS over plain equity?",a:"CCPS offer: (1) liquidation preference -- paid before equity in downside; (2) fixed dividend preference; (3) anti-dilution protection via adjustable conversion ratios; (4) FEMA compliance -- treated as equity for FDI (compulsory conversion); (5) valuation flexibility; (6) optionality for different exit economics. Company avoids immediate voting dilution until conversion.",topic:"PE / VC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA","NDA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:48,q:"What are anti-dilution rights? Explain broad-based and narrow-based weighted average.",a:"Anti-dilution protects investors from down-round dilution. Full ratchet: conversion price drops to new lower price -- maximum investor protection, punitive for founders. Weighted average: adjusts based on shares issued at lower price. Narrow-based: only outstanding shares in denominator. Broad-based: includes options, warrants, convertibles -- larger denominator means less adjustment (founder-friendly). BBWA is market standard.",topic:"PE / VC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA","NDA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:49,q:"What is the difference between ROFR and ROFO?",a:"ROFR: seller gets a third-party offer first, then offers shares to ROFR holder on same terms -- holder matches or declines. ROFO: seller must offer to ROFO holder first before approaching third parties -- holder bids first, only if declined can seller seek external offers at equal or higher price. ROFR gives more protection; ROFO gives first-mover advantage. ROFR more common in Indian SHAs.",topic:"PE / VC",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA","NDA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:50,q:"What are liquidation preferences? Participating vs. non-participating.",a:"Non-participating: investor gets 1x investment back OR converts to equity (choose one). Participating: investor gets preference amount AND participates pro-rata in remainder (double-dipping). 1x non-participating is Indian market standard; participating is used in later-stage/distressed deals. Participating preferences significantly reduce founder upside.",topic:"PE / VC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA","NDA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:51,q:"Walk me through the process of an IPO in India.",a:"Key stages: (1) Appoint intermediaries (BRLMs, legal counsel, auditors); (2) DRHP preparation with comprehensive disclosure; (3) DRHP filed with SEBI -- 30-75 day review; (4) Filed with ROC/exchanges; (5) Roadshows and anchor allocation; (6) Book-building with QIB/NII/retail bidding; (7) Allotment; (8) Listing. Requirements: minimum offer size, 25% public float, lock-ins, ICDR track record requirements.",topic:"Capital Markets",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Trilegal"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:52,q:"What is UPSI under SEBI PIT Regulations?",a:"Unpublished Price Sensitive Information: information not generally available that would materially affect securities price. Examples: financial results, dividends, capital structure changes, M&A, key personnel changes, auditor changes. Trading while possessing UPSI creates a presumption of insider trading. Companies must maintain structured digital databases and comply with trading window restrictions.",topic:"Capital Markets",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Trilegal"],track:"Corporate",detailed:false},
{id:53,q:"What triggers a mandatory open offer under SEBI SAST Regulations?",a:"Three triggers: (1) Acquisition of 25%+ voting rights (initial threshold); (2) Creeping acquisition -- 25-75% holder acquiring 5%+ in a financial year; (3) Acquisition of control regardless of shareholding. Open offer must be for 26%+ of total shares at SAST pricing (highest of agreed price, VWAP, negotiated price). Exemptions for inter-se transfers, rights issues, IBC acquisitions.",topic:"Capital Markets",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:54,q:"Distinguish between mortgage, pledge, and hypothecation.",a:"Mortgage (TPA): immovable property -- interest transferred without possession transfer (except usufructuary). Pledge (ICA S.172): movable property -- possession transfers to lender, who can sell on default after notice. Hypothecation: movable property -- borrower retains possession, charge created for lender (vehicles, inventory). Under SARFAESI, secured creditors can enforce without court for debts above Rs.20 lakhs.",topic:"Banking & Finance",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","JSA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:55,q:"What is the difference between a fixed charge and a floating charge?",a:"Fixed charge: attaches to specific identified asset -- borrower cannot deal without lender consent. Floating charge: hovers over a changing class of assets (inventory, receivables) -- borrower deals freely until crystallisation (on default, receiver appointment, winding up). Fixed holders rank above floating in priority. Both must be registered with ROC within 30 days.",topic:"Banking & Finance",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","JSA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:56,q:"What is the SARFAESI Act?",a:"Empowers banks/FIs to enforce security without court intervention. On default: 60-day notice, then creditor can take possession, sell/lease assets, appoint manager, require acquirers to pay debt. Borrower appeals to DRT. Does not apply to: security under Rs.20 lakhs, agricultural land, or unpaid amount below 20% of principal+interest.",topic:"Banking & Finance",companies:["CAM","AZB","Khaitan","JSA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:57,q:"FDI automatic route vs. government approval route?",a:"Automatic: no prior approval needed, file FC-GPR post-facto within 30 days. Sectors like IT, manufacturing, e-commerce marketplace allow 100% automatic. Government: prior ministry approval required -- multi-brand retail (51%), media (varies), defence (above 74%). Certain sectors prohibit FDI entirely (gambling, lottery, atomic energy). Governed by FDI Policy read with FEMA NDI Rules 2019.",topic:"FEMA",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA","NDA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:58,q:"Tell me about FC-TRS, FC-GPR, and DI forms.",a:"FC-GPR: filed on fresh share issuance to non-resident -- 30 days from allotment. FC-TRS: filed on share transfer between resident and non-resident -- 60 days from transfer. Form DI: filed when an Indian entity owned/controlled by non-residents makes downstream investment. All filed on RBI FIRMS portal. Non-compliance attracts FEMA penalties/compounding.",topic:"FEMA",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA","NDA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:59,q:"What are anti-competitive agreements under Section 3 of the Competition Act?",a:"Section 3 prohibits agreements causing AAEC. Horizontal (between competitors): price fixing, market allocation, bid rigging, output limitation -- presumed anti-competitive (per se void under S.3(3)). Vertical (different supply chain levels): tie-ins, exclusive supply/distribution, refusal to deal, RPM -- rule of reason analysis. 2023 Amendment expanded scope with deal-value threshold and material influence control definition.",topic:"Competition Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:60,q:"Explain the Competition (Amendment) Act, 2023 deal-value threshold.",a:"Key changes: (1) Deal-value threshold -- transactions exceeding Rs.2,000 crore with target having substantial business operations in India must notify CCI regardless of asset/turnover thresholds. Captures digital/asset-light acquisitions. (2) Control broadened to material influence (lower than prior decisive influence). (3) Settlement/commitment mechanisms introduced. (4) Reduced combination limitation period.",topic:"Competition Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:61,q:"Is a non-compete clause enforceable under Indian law?",a:"Post-employment non-competes are void under Section 27 ICA (restraint of trade) -- regardless of duration, geography, or compensation. During-employment non-competes are enforceable. Non-solicitation and confidentiality obligations survive termination and are generally enforceable if reasonable. Garden leave clauses are a practical workaround with limited judicial testing.",topic:"Employment Law",companies:["Trilegal","SAM","CAM","NDA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:62,q:"What are the POSH Act obligations for employers?",a:"Employers with 10+ employees must: constitute ICC (presiding senior woman, 2+ employee members, 1 external NGO member), provide safe environment, display penal consequences, organise workshops, include POSH in service rules, file annual district report. Complaints within 3 months; ICC inquiry within 90 days; action recommended within 10 days.",topic:"Employment Law",companies:["Trilegal","SAM","CAM","NDA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:false},
{id:63,q:"What is a slump sale and its tax implications?",a:"Transfer of undertaking as going concern for lump sum without individual asset valuation (Section 2(42C) IT Act). Capital gain = sale consideration minus net worth of undertaking. Long-term or short-term based on holding period. GST may apply. Advantage: simplicity and potentially lower stamp duty vs. itemised transfer.",topic:"Tax",companies:["Khaitan","ELP","CAM","SAM","AZB","NDA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:64,q:"What are the types of companies under the Companies Act, 2013?",a:"By liability: limited by shares, guarantee, unlimited. By members: private (2-200, restricted transfers) and public (7+, freely transferable). Others: OPC, Section 8 (not-for-profit), government company (51%+ govt), holding/subsidiary, associate, small companies. Private must have Private Limited; public must have Limited. Distinction affects director requirements, audit committee, compliance.",topic:"Company Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:65,q:"What is the difference between MoA and AoA?",a:"MoA: charter document defining company's relationship with outside world -- name, registered office, objects, liability, authorised capital. Sets powers boundaries. AoA: internal rules -- share transfers, board meetings, dividends, management. MoA is supreme; contradicting AoA provisions are void. Third parties have constructive notice of MoA (ultra vires) but not AoA (indoor management/Turquand's rule). SHA overrides AoA between parties, but AoA governs the company.",topic:"Company Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:66,q:"What are related party transactions and the approval process?",a:"RPTs are transactions between company and directors, KMPs, promoters, relatives, subsidiaries. Section 188: material RPTs need Board approval (interested directors abstain) and shareholder approval if exceeding thresholds. For listed companies, SEBI LODR adds audit committee and shareholder approval, arm's length requirement. SEBI LODR's related party definition is wider than Companies Act.",topic:"Company Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal"],track:"Corporate",detailed:false},
{id:67,q:"What is oppression and mismanagement under Sections 241-242?",a:"Petition before NCLT by members with 1/10th issued capital (or 1/5th of members). Must show affairs conducted prejudicially/oppressively, or material management change likely prejudicial. NCLT powers under S.242: regulate conduct, order share purchase, restrict AoA alteration, remove/appoint directors. Primary minority protection remedy; critical in PE/VC disputes.",topic:"Company Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Both",detailed:true},
{id:68,q:"What is vesting and reverse vesting in an SHA?",a:"Vesting: founder/employee earns ownership over time -- typical 4-year schedule with 1-year cliff (25% at cliff, rest monthly/quarterly). Incentivises long-term commitment. Reverse vesting: applied to founders already holding shares -- shares subject to company repurchase right that lapses over time. If founder leaves early, unvested shares bought back at cost/nominal price. SHA specifies acceleration events (change of control).",topic:"PE / VC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","NDA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:69,q:"Preferential allotment vs. private placement?",a:"Preferential allotment (S.62(1)(c)): issue to select group on preferential basis -- special resolution, pricing guidelines (SEBI ICDR for listed). Private placement (S.42): offer to up to 200 identified persons per offer (excl. QIBs/ESOP employees) -- special resolution, private placement offer letter, no public advertisement. Both alternatives to rights issues/public offers for specific investor capital raising.",topic:"Company Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal"],track:"Corporate",detailed:false},
{id:70,q:"Limitation periods for money recovery, specific performance, and declaration?",a:"Recovery of money on contract: 3 years from right to sue (Article 55). Specific performance: 3 years from date fixed for performance or notice of refusal (Article 54). Declaration: 3 years. Section 5 condones delay in appeals/applications (not suits) for sufficient cause. Acknowledgment under S.18 creates fresh limitation. B.K. Educational Services: Limitation Act applies to IBC Section 7.",topic:"Limitation & Property",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:71,q:"What is adverse possession?",a:"Acquiring title by continuous, open, hostile, uninterrupted possession for 12 years (private property) or 30 years (government property). Possession must be without owner's permission, open/visible, with animus possidendi. Burden on claimant. On expiry, owner's right extinguished, adverse possessor gets fresh title. SC in Ravinder Kaur Grewal criticised it but acknowledged it as settled law.",topic:"Limitation & Property",companies:["CAM","Khaitan","Karanjawala"],track:"Litigation",detailed:false},
{id:72,q:"Merger vs. demerger vs. slump sale?",a:"Merger: two companies combine via NCLT scheme (S.230-232), 6-12 months, can be tax-neutral (S.2(1B), S.47). Demerger: splitting undertaking via NCLT scheme. Slump sale: BTA transferring undertaking as going concern for lump sum -- contractual, faster, no NCLT. Tax: merger potentially neutral; slump sale taxed under S.50B. Stamp duty differs by structure and state.",topic:"M&A",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA","ELP"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:73,q:"What is GAAR?",a:"General Anti-Avoidance Rules (Chapter X-A IT Act): declares an arrangement an impermissible avoidance arrangement if main purpose is tax benefit and it creates non-arm's length rights, misuses provisions, lacks commercial substance, or is not bona fide. Can disregard arrangement, reallocate income, re-characterise transactions, deny treaty benefits. Applies when tax benefit exceeds Rs.3 crores. Effective AY 2018-19. Residual -- SAAR prevails where applicable.",topic:"Tax",companies:["Khaitan","ELP","NDA","CAM","AZB"],track:"Corporate",detailed:false},
{id:74,q:"Term sheet vs. definitive documents?",a:"Term sheet outlines deal structure and commercial understanding -- typically non-binding except for confidentiality, exclusivity, costs, and governing law clauses. Definitive documents (SHA, SPA, SSA) are binding, exhaustive contracts incorporating detailed representations, conditions precedent, and indemnities. Signing definitive agreements creates legal obligations, though closing remains subject to CPs.",topic:"M&A",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA","NDA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:false},
{id:75,q:"What are Conditions Precedent (CPs) and Conditions Subsequent (CSs)?",a:"CPs: conditions that must be fulfilled or waived after signing but BEFORE closing (e.g., CCI approval, corporate resolutions, third-party consents). Transaction cannot close until CPs are satisfied. CSs: obligations to be fulfilled AFTER closing within a specified timeline (e.g., post-closing filings like Form FC-GPR). Breach of CP prevents deal from closing; breach of CS triggers indemnity or termination claims.",topic:"M&A",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA","NDA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:false},
{id:76,q:"What is a reverse break fee?",a:"A fee payable by the buyer to the seller if the transaction terminates due to reasons attributable to the buyer -- e.g., failure to secure financing or failure to get internal approvals. Functions as liquidated damage for the seller's lost time and expenses. Standard break fee is paid by seller to buyer; reverse break fee protects the seller. Must be reasonable under Section 74 ICA.",topic:"M&A",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:false},
{id:77,q:"Ratio decidendi vs. obiter dicta?",a:"Ratio decidendi: the core legal principle determining the case outcome -- binding precedent under Article 141. Obiter dicta: incidental remarks not essential to the decision -- persuasive but not binding. Identifying ratio requires isolating material facts, legal issue, and principle applied. SC decisions bind all courts; HC decisions bind subordinate courts in that state. Larger bench overrules smaller bench.",topic:"Constitutional Law",companies:["Karanjawala","Agarwal Law Associates","Parekh & Co.","CAM","SAM"],track:"Litigation",detailed:false},
{id:78,q:"What is a curative petition?",a:"Last judicial remedy -- established in Rupa Ashok Hurra (2002), filed after review dismissal. Narrow grounds: violation of natural justice, judge bias, abuse of process. Must be certified by a senior advocate. Circulated to three senior-most judges and original bench -- if merit found, heard in open court. Succeeds very rarely.",topic:"Constitutional Law",companies:["Karanjawala","Agarwal Law Associates","Parekh & Co."],track:"Litigation",detailed:false},
{id:79,q:"Financial creditor vs. operational creditor under IBC?",a:"Financial creditor (S.5(7)): owed financial debt (disbursed against time value of money -- loans, bonds, debentures, lease finance). Operational creditor (S.5(20)): owed operational debt (goods, services, employment, government dues). Key differences: FC has CoC voting rights; OC has none. FC files under S.7 (lower threshold); OC under S.9 (must show no pre-existing dispute). Upheld as constitutional in Swiss Ribbons.",topic:"IBC",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Both",detailed:true},
{id:80,q:"Specific performance after the 2018 Specific Relief Act Amendment?",a:"Section 10 now mandates specific performance as default (shall be enforced), no longer discretionary. Court cannot refuse solely because damages are adequate remedy. Exceptions are narrow (S.11, S.14, S.16) -- such as contracts involving personal skill, determinable contracts, or where the plaintiff is not ready and willing. Shifted risk allocation dramatically in commercial contracts.",topic:"Contract Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Both",detailed:false},
{id:81,q:"What is the standard of proof in civil vs. criminal cases?",a:"Civil: preponderance of probabilities -- the court weighs whether a fact is more likely true than not. Criminal: beyond reasonable doubt -- prosecution must prove guilt so conclusively that no rational alternative hypothesis exists. Higher threshold in criminal matters protects personal liberty. Exceptions exist in special statutes with reverse burden (e.g., PMLA S.45, NDPS).",topic:"Evidence",companies:["CAM","SAM","Khaitan","Karanjawala"],track:"Litigation",detailed:false},
{id:82,q:"What is downstream investment under FEMA?",a:"Investment made by an Indian entity (which has foreign investment and is owned or controlled by non-residents) into another Indian entity. Treated as indirect foreign investment in the recipient -- must comply with FDI sectoral conditions. File Form DI on FIRMS portal within 30 days. Whether the investing company is owned and controlled by residents determines if its investment counts as foreign or domestic -- critical in capped sectors.",topic:"FEMA",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA","NDA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:83,q:"Explain the concept of control under Companies Act vs. SEBI SAST vs. FEMA.",a:"Companies Act (S.2(27)): right to appoint majority of directors or control management/policy decisions. SEBI SAST: identical definition, but interpreted expansively -- veto rights on material corporate changes can imply control (Subhkam case, later adjusted). FEMA: right to appoint majority directors or control management/policy. Veto rights for minority protection generally do not constitute control if confined to protective matters.",topic:"Company Law",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:false},
{id:84,q:"What are the essentials of an indemnification clause?",a:"Must specify: (1) scope -- which losses covered; (2) caps -- ceiling on total liability (10-100% of deal value); (3) baskets -- de minimis per claim and aggregate basket before indemnity payable; (4) time limitation -- survival period (12-24 months general, longer for fundamental warranties); (5) procedure -- notice, defence rights, cooperation; (6) exclusions -- consequential damages, lost profit. Buyers want broad/high/low/long; sellers want narrow/low/high/short.",topic:"M&A",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA","NDA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:85,q:"Pre-emptive rights vs. anti-dilution rights?",a:"Pre-emptive (S.62(1)(a)): right to subscribe to new shares pro-rata before outsiders -- maintains percentage ownership. Anti-dilution (contractual in SHA): protects economic value per share -- conversion ratio adjusted if company issues at lower price (down-round). Pre-emptive prevents percentage dilution; anti-dilution prevents valuation/economic dilution.",topic:"PE / VC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","NDA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:false},
{id:86,q:"What is a material contract in an M&A diligence context?",a:"Contracts exceeding specified threshold (e.g., 5% of revenue), joint venture agreements, financing/loan documents, contracts with change-of-control clauses, exclusivity agreements, or non-competes restricting operations. Diligence checks for: assignment restrictions, termination penalties, liabilities, and financial covenants that might trigger on the transaction.",topic:"M&A",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Corporate",detailed:false},
{id:87,q:"Can an investor claim specific performance of an unsigned term sheet?",a:"Generally no. An unsigned term sheet lacks expression of mutual consent and execution. Even if signed, if labeled non-binding, it cannot be specifically enforced under Specific Relief Act -- except for binding clauses like exclusivity or confidentiality. Remedy for breach of exclusivity would be damages (loss of the investment terms) or reliance damages (costs incurred in negotiation). The practical challenge is proving quantum of loss from a deal that never closed.",topic:"Contract Law",companies:["General"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:88,q:"An SPA has a hell-or-high-water closing obligation. The seller argues frustration under Section 56 ICA because regulatory approval was refused. Evaluate.",a:"Frustration under Section 56 requires impossibility independent of the parties' acts. Where a party has contractually assumed regulatory risk (hell-or-high-water clause), it cannot invoke frustration for the very risk it accepted -- Satyabrata Ghose v. Mugneeram. A MAC clause is a separate termination right, not frustration. The practical difference: a CP failure allows termination under the contract's own terms (no damages); frustration discharges the contract entirely (restitution under Section 65). Where regulatory risk is allocated by the contract, the correct route is the CP/MAC mechanism, not frustration.",topic:"Contract Law",companies:["General"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:89,q:"Escrow holdback released at 18 months; warranty claim arises at month 14 but notified after release. Can the buyer recover?",a:"Key distinction: the survival period (time to make a claim) is separate from the escrow period (time to fund the claim from escrow). If the buyer validly notified the claim within the survival period (month 14 is within 18 months), the claim is preserved. The release of the escrow money merely means the buyer loses the secured source of recovery -- they can still sue the seller directly for breach of warranty under the contract, subject to the overall limitation/survival boundaries specified in the SPA.",topic:"M&A",companies:["General"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:90,q:"SPA has anti-sandbagging clause. Buyer discovers regulatory non-compliance during DD but signs anyway. Can buyer claim indemnity post-closing?",a:"No. Anti-sandbagging prohibits the buyer from bringing a warranty/indemnity claim for a breach they knew about prior to signing/closing. Since the buyer discovered the non-compliance during due diligence, their claim is barred by the anti-sandbagging clause. The buyer should have structured this as a specific indemnity or a pre-closing CP, rather than relying on general warranties. Pro-sandbagging would allow the claim -- Indian law does not have a settled position; it depends on how the SPA is drafted. Fraudulent concealment (Section 17 ICA) overrides any contractual limitation.",topic:"M&A",companies:["General"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:91,q:"A founder gives a personal indemnity in an SSA. Three years later the company is wound up. Can investors enforce against the founder personally?",a:"Yes. A personal indemnity creates a direct contractual obligation between the founder (indemnifier) and the investors (indemnified) under Sections 124-125 ICA. The company's dissolution does not extinguish the founder's personal obligation -- it is a separate contract. The indemnity claim survives the winding up because the founder, not the company, is the obligor. The limitation period runs from when the loss crystalised (typically the breach event or discovery), not from the company's winding up.",topic:"Contract Law",companies:["General"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:92,q:"SHA provides Rs.10 crore liquidated damages for lock-in breach; actual loss is Rs.1 crore. Is the clause enforceable?",a:"India follows Sections 73-74 ICA -- reasonable compensation, not the strict English penalty/LD distinction. Post-ONGC v. Saw Pipes and Kailash Nath Associates, Indian courts award actual loss even where an LD clause exists, unless a genuine pre-estimate is established. The court would likely cap recovery at actual loss (Rs.1 crore) because the named sum is heavily disproportionate and penal. The burden is on the plaintiff to prove loss, unless proving loss is genuinely impossible.",topic:"Contract Law",companies:["General"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:93,q:"Corporate guarantee covers all dues of OpCo. OpCo defaults. Lender sues Guarantor directly without suing OpCo. Maintainable?",a:"Yes. Under Section 128 ICA, the liability of a surety is co-extensive with that of the principal debtor, unless otherwise provided by the contract. The lender is under no legal obligation to exhaust remedies against the principal debtor (OpCo) before proceeding against the guarantor. The lender can sue the guarantor directly for the entire amount upon default. Indian guarantees are typically on demand, not conditional on exhaustion.",topic:"Contract Law",companies:["General"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:94,q:"A startup proposes to issue shares to 70 investors as a rights issue to avoid Section 42 private placement limits. Is this valid?",a:"Invalid. Rights issue (Section 62(1)(a)) requires shares to be offered pro-rata to existing shareholders first -- an offer to 70 new investors cannot be a rights issue. This is a sham structuring to circumvent the Section 42 private placement requirement (identified persons cap per tranche). Regulatory consequence: RoC can treat it as a non-compliant private placement, attracting penalties under Section 42.",topic:"Company Law",companies:["General"],track:"Corporate",detailed:true},
{id:95,q:"Company fails to file private placement return (PAS-3) within 15 days. What are the legal consequences?",a:"Consequences: (a) company and directors liable to a penalty -- amount raised through the invalid placement or Rs.2 crore, whichever is lower (Section 42(10)); (b) the allotment may be void; (c) company must repay subscription money within 30 days with interest at 12% p.a.; (d) RoC can initiate prosecution; (e) for listed companies, SEBI can impose additional penalties. The consequences are severe enough that compliance is non-negotiable.",topic:"Company Law",companies:["General"],track:"Corporate",detailed:false},
{id:140,q:"Companies Act cap on total managerial remuneration as percentage of net profits?",a:"Section 197: total managerial remuneration cannot exceed 11% of net profits of the company. Within this: an MD/whole-time director cannot receive more than 5% individually (or 10% for all MDs/WTDs collectively); non-executive directors cannot receive more than 1% (if MD exists) or 3% (if no MD). If the company has no profits or inadequate profits, Schedule V prescribes the maximum remuneration payable without central government approval. Shareholder approval (special resolution) is needed for remuneration exceeding prescribed limits.",topic:"Company Law",companies:["General"],track:"Corporate",detailed:false},
{id:141,q:"Category II AIF minimum corpus and minimum investor commitment?",a:"Under SEBI AIF Regulations, 2012: Category II AIF (which includes most PE/VC funds) requires a minimum corpus of Rs.20 crore. Minimum investment by each investor is Rs.1 crore. The fund manager/sponsor must contribute at least 2.5% of the corpus or Rs.5 crore (whichever is lower) as skin in the game. Maximum number of investors per scheme is 1,000. Tenure is determined by the fund documents but must be specified at inception.",topic:"PE / VC",companies:["General"],track:"Corporate",detailed:false},

// ── CPC ADVANCED QUESTIONS (from PDF) ────────────────────────────────────
{id:200,q:"What is constructive res judicata (Explanation IV to Section 11)? Illustrate with an example.",a:"Constructive res judicata bars a party from raising in a subsequent proceeding any ground of attack or defence which was available to it in the former proceeding but was not taken. The rationale is finality of litigation. Example: if A sues B for rent and B could have raised a set-off but did not, B cannot raise that set-off as a cause of action in a fresh suit. In writ proceedings, if a party challenges an order on certain grounds and later tries to challenge the same order in a fresh writ on different grounds, constructive res judicata bars the second writ. It also applies in execution proceedings -- issues of satisfaction of a decree cannot be re-agitated if they could have been raised in the earlier execution application.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","SAM","Karanjawala"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:201,q:"Explain the foreign judgment exceptions under Section 13 CPC. How are decrees from non-reciprocating territories enforced?",a:"Section 13 provides six exceptions where a foreign judgment is not conclusive: (1) not pronounced by a court of competent jurisdiction; (2) not on the merits (e.g., ex parte without proper service); (3) based on incorrect view of international law or refusal to recognise Indian law; (4) obtained by fraud; (5) breach of natural justice; (6) founded on breach of Indian law. Section 44A allows direct execution of decrees from 'reciprocating territories' (notified by Central Government -- e.g., UK, Singapore). Decrees from non-reciprocating territories (e.g., USA, most Middle East countries) must be sued upon in India as a fresh suit, with the foreign judgment being only evidence of the debt -- limitation is three years from the date of the foreign decree.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:202,q:"Explain Order 38 Rules 5-13 (attachment before judgment) and why most applications fail.",a:"Order 38 Rule 5 allows the court to attach property if it is shown that the defendant is about to dispose of or remove property with intent to obstruct execution of a decree. The standard requires specific evidence of both: (i) a prima facie case AND (ii) actual intent to defraud or obstruct -- mere apprehension is insufficient; bald allegations of disposal are not enough. Courts require specific evidence of actual attempts to alienate, fraudulent transfers, or sudden asset movements. Most applications fail because lawyers file on vague apprehension without concrete evidence. The remedy is discretionary and courts are cautious given its drastic nature. On a successful application, the defendant can furnish security in lieu of attachment.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","SAM","Karanjawala"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:203,q:"Explain Order 9 Rule 13 -- what must the defendant show to set aside an ex parte decree?",a:"The defendant must show 'sufficient cause' for non-appearance on the date of hearing. The Supreme Court has held that sufficient cause alone is sufficient -- there is no separate requirement to show a meritorious defence. However, courts do look at the merits for framing their relief. If summons was duly served but counsel failed to appear, courts generally hold the party responsible. The Explanation to Order 9 Rule 13 provides that where the summons was not duly served, sufficient cause need not be shown. An ex parte decree can also be challenged by a first appeal under Section 96(2) simultaneously -- both remedies are available in parallel. Limitation: 30 days from decree if summons was duly served; 30 days from knowledge if not.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","Khaitan","SAM","Karanjawala","JSA"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:204,q:"Explain Order 39 Rule 3A -- the 30-day rule for deciding injunction applications.",a:"Order 39 Rule 3A provides that where an ex parte injunction has been granted, the application must be disposed of within 30 days from the date of grant. If the court fails to comply, the injunction stands vacated unless specifically extended for reasons recorded. This prevents indefinite ex parte injunctions. In practice, the 30-day period is often not complied with. The remedy: file an application pointing out the Rule 3A default and seeking vacation of the injunction. The court may extend time if it records reasons. From the applicant's perspective: file a mention to advance the hearing date, or apply for extension before expiry with specific reasons.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","SAM","Karanjawala"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:205,q:"Can an executing court go behind the decree? What are the exceptions?",a:"The general rule is that an executing court cannot go behind the decree -- it must execute the decree as drawn (Vasudevan v. Pattammal). The exceptions are: (1) if the court that passed the decree lacked inherent jurisdiction (not merely irregular exercise of jurisdiction), the decree is a nullity and the executing court can ignore it; (2) fraud going to jurisdiction -- in certain cases the executing court can look into allegations of fraud that affect jurisdiction; (3) where the decree itself is ambiguous and requires construction -- the executing court can interpret but not modify. The distinction between 'inherent lack of jurisdiction' (nullity, executing court can refuse) and 'irregular exercise of jurisdiction' (voidable, only challenge is by appeal) is critical.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Khaitan","Karanjawala","JSA"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:206,q:"Explain Order 6 Rule 17 -- amendment of pleadings post-commencement of trial.",a:"Order 6 Rule 17 allows the court to permit amendment at any stage on terms as may be just. The 2002 proviso adds that after commencement of trial, no amendment shall be permitted unless the court concludes that the party despite due diligence could not have raised the matter before commencement. An amendment cannot be allowed if it: introduces a new case changing the nature of the suit; takes away an accrued defence of limitation (time-barred on the amendment date but not at original filing); or is fraudulent. The doctrine of 'relation back' means an amendment dates back to the date of the original suit for limitation purposes -- hence the controversy about limitation defences being defeated by amendments.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA","Karanjawala"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:207,q:"What is the 120-day rule for written statements in commercial suits?",a:"Under the Commercial Courts Act amendment to Order VIII Rule 1, a defendant in a commercial suit must file a written statement within 30 days of service of summons. The court may extend this to 120 days for good cause. Beyond 120 days, the right to file a written statement is permanently forfeited -- the 120-day outer limit is mandatory, not directory (Kailash v. Nanhku distinguished). The court has no jurisdiction to extend time beyond 120 days even with parties' consent. Counterclaims are similarly time-bound. The forfeiture of the right to file written statement does not mean the defendant cannot participate -- they can still cross-examine.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:208,q:"Explain mandatory pre-institution mediation under Section 12A Commercial Courts Act.",a:"Section 12A requires parties to exhaust pre-institution mediation before filing a commercial suit -- unless the suit contemplates urgent interim relief. Mediation is conducted by the DLSA or other notified bodies within a 3-month period (extendable by 2 months). Non-compliance renders the plaint liable to rejection under Order VII Rule 11(d) as a suit barred by law. The exception for urgent interim relief means a plaintiff seeking an ex parte injunction can bypass mediation. The Supreme Court in Patil Automation clarified the mandatory nature and that non-compliance is fatal to the suit. Failure to attempt mediation cannot be condoned later.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:209,q:"What is summary judgment under Order 13A Commercial Courts Act? How does it differ from Order 12 Rule 6 and Order 37?",a:"Order 13A allows a party to apply for summary judgment if the other party has 'no real prospect of succeeding' on their claim or defence -- the English CPR standard. Order 12 Rule 6 (judgment on admissions) requires the other side to have made a clear admission in pleadings or documents -- narrower and based on what has been conceded. Order 37 (summary suits) is available only for specific categories (bills of exchange, written contracts for money) and requires the defendant to obtain leave to defend. Key distinction: Order 13A can be used at any stage, is not category-restricted, and applies the 'no real prospect' standard rather than requiring an admission or specific instrument. It is broader and more versatile than both.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:210,q:"Explain Section 115 CPC (revision) post-2002 amendment. How does Article 227 fill the gap?",a:"Post-2002, Section 115 was significantly narrowed: the High Court can exercise revision only if the order would finally dispose of the suit or proceedings -- it cannot interfere with interlocutory orders unless they have this effect. This created a gap: many interlocutory orders causing serious prejudice (e.g., wrongful rejection of evidence, refusal of discovery) could no longer be revised. Article 227 of the Constitution (superintendence over all courts) fills this gap -- it is wider than Section 115 and not subject to the same restrictions. High Courts routinely entertain Article 227 petitions against interlocutory orders. The distinction matters: Article 227 is a constitutional power, not revisional jurisdiction, and is exercised more cautiously than the old Section 115.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","Karanjawala","Parekh & Co."],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:211,q:"Explain Section 89 CPC court-referred ADR. What did Afcons Infrastructure clarify?",a:"Section 89 requires courts to refer disputes to ADR if it appears that settlement may be possible. Five modes: arbitration, conciliation, judicial settlement (including Lok Adalat), mediation, Lok Adalat. The Supreme Court in Afcons Infrastructure v. Cherian Varkey Construction identified drafting errors in Section 89 -- the court held that arbitration and conciliation under Section 89 require the consent of both parties (unlike mediation/judicial settlement). The court cannot compel parties into binding arbitration without consent. The court must record reasons for the mode chosen. Unsuitable cases (e.g., criminal cases on private complaints, matrimonial cases involving serious offences) should not be referred.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Trilegal","SAM","Karanjawala"],track:"Litigation",detailed:false},
{id:212,q:"Explain Section 151 CPC -- inherent powers and their limits. Give three practical uses.",a:"Section 151 preserves the inherent powers of the court to make orders necessary to meet the ends of justice or prevent abuse of process. It is residuary -- cannot be invoked where the Code expressly covers the situation or where it would be contrary to express provisions. Three practical uses: (1) recalling an order obtained by suppression of material facts (fraud on court); (2) staying proceedings to prevent multiplicity when Section 10 does not apply (e.g., different cause of action but same factual matrix); (3) granting an extension of time beyond the Code's limits in exceptional circumstances where express provision is silent. Section 151 cannot: restore a suit dismissed in default (Order 9 has its own provisions); condone delay in filing written statement beyond 120-day commercial limit; override bar under Order 23 Rule 1 on fresh suits after withdrawal without liberty.",topic:"CPC",companies:["CAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","SAM","Karanjawala"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:213,q:"You receive a brief at 6 PM -- client's property is being demolished tomorrow morning. Walk me through everything you do.",a:"Immediate steps: (1) Check caveat -- search court registry for any existing caveat under Section 148A CPC; if a caveator exists, they must be heard before any ex parte order is passed. (2) Gather documents: title deeds, cause of action material, identity proof; calculate court fees and stamp duty. (3) Draft plaint with prayer clause specifically seeking ad-interim injunction under Order XXXIX Rules 1 and 2, plus application under Order XXXIX Rule 3 with supporting affidavit. (4) Calculate jurisdiction: territorial (where property situate) and pecuniary. (5) Mentioning before appropriate bench next morning -- prepare a mentioning note explaining urgency. (6) At 10:30 AM: appear before the court, explain urgency, seek ex parte ad-interim injunction; be ready with authority on the three-prong test (prima facie, balance of convenience, irreparable injury). Document advice and probability assessment given to client -- at least 40% candidly assessed.",topic:"CPC",companies:["General","Karanjawala","Parekh & Co.","Agarwal Law Associates"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:214,q:"Your client was set ex parte three months ago and a decree was passed last week. Map every remedy with its limitation period.",a:"Three remedies available in parallel: (1) Order 9 Rule 13 (set aside ex parte decree) -- application to the trial court; limitation: 30 days from date of decree if summons was duly served; from date of knowledge if not duly served. Sufficient cause for non-appearance must be shown. (2) First appeal under Section 96(2) -- an ex parte decree is appealable; limitation: 30 days for District Court decrees, 90 days for High Court decrees. The appellate court on first appeal has wider powers than the trial court on Rule 13. (3) Revision under Article 227 / Section 115 -- available if the ex parte decree was passed without jurisdiction or in violation of natural justice. Order 9 Rule 13 and Section 96(2) appeal can run simultaneously -- the Explanation to Rule 13 clarifies that success in appeal does not bar future Rule 13 applications, but the court may stay the Rule 13 application pending appeal.",topic:"CPC",companies:["General","Karanjawala","Parekh & Co."],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:215,q:"The defendant is selling assets while your money suit is pending. What is your remedy and what evidence do you put on affidavit?",a:"Order 38 Rule 5 (attachment before judgment) is the primary remedy. On affidavit, you must establish: (1) a prima facie case; (2) the defendant is about to dispose of or remove assets from jurisdiction; (3) this is done with intent to obstruct or defeat execution. Most applications fail because lawyers plead general apprehension. On affidavit include: suspicious transfers in recent weeks, selling of specific assets, statements made by the defendant about disposal, sudden opening of accounts in other jurisdictions, bank transactions showing asset movement. Alternatively, if there is a contract with an anti-alienation clause, an injunction under Order XXXIX Rule 2 is more appropriate. For interim protection while the Order 38 application is pending, seek a short ad-interim order at the same time. Remember: attachment before judgment is only a security for the decree -- the attached property still belongs to the defendant.",topic:"CPC",companies:["General","Karanjawala","Parekh & Co.","Agarwal Law Associates"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
// ── BSA QUESTIONS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{id:220,q:"From what date is the BSA in force, and does it apply to pending proceedings?",a:"The BSA came into force on 1 July 2024 (Section 170(1)). Section 170(2) provides that the old Indian Evidence Act 1872 (IEA) continues to apply to all trials, appeals, and other proceedings pending on the date of commencement -- i.e., proceedings commenced before 1 July 2024 continue under the IEA. Fresh proceedings commenced on or after 1 July 2024 are governed by the BSA. An appeal filed after 1 July 2024 against a pre-BSA conviction is governed by the IEA since the proceedings were commenced before 1 July 2024. 'Pending' is interpreted as at the point evidence begins to be recorded, not at the appeal stage.",topic:"BSA",companies:["Khaitan","CAM","SAM","JSA","Karanjawala"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:221,q:"What are three substantive changes in the BSA compared to the Indian Evidence Act 1872?",a:"Three significant substantive changes: (1) Electronic records -- Section 57 now has seven Explanations addressing simultaneous/sequential storage, cloud environments, and automatic temporary files, clarifying when an electronic record is 'primary evidence.' The certificate requirement under Section 63 replaces old Section 65B with a dual-signature requirement and a Part A/Part B Schedule format including hash values for integrity. (2) Section 23(1) and 23(2) merge old Sections 25-26 (confessions to police and in police custody) into one section, and adds 'coercion' to the list of vitiating factors. (3) The definition of 'document' in Section 2(1)(d) expressly includes server logs, locational evidence, and voice mails, expanding what can be filed as documentary evidence. Additionally, Section 63(4) requires the certificate 'at each instance' the electronic record is tendered.",topic:"BSA",companies:["Khaitan","CAM","SAM","JSA","Karanjawala"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:222,q:"Explain Section 63 BSA (electronic records certificate) -- how does it differ from old Section 65B IEA?",a:"Section 63 BSA replaces old Section 65B IEA with several changes: (1) Dual-signature requirement -- the certificate must now be signed by the person in charge of the computer AND a responsible official. Old Section 65B required only one signatory. (2) 'At each instance' -- the certificate must be submitted each time the electronic record is tendered, not just once. (3) Schedule format -- Part A covers device particulars, lawful control, and hash values (SHA1/SHA256/MD5); Part B is for expert analysis. (4) Hash value requirement -- for integrity verification. (5) Section 63(3) deems multiple computers, networks, cloud resources, and intermediaries as 'a single computer' -- addressing modern cloud architectures. The Arjun Panditrao Khotkar mandatory certificate requirement is retained -- the certificate is necessary for secondary electronic evidence, not optional.",topic:"BSA",companies:["Khaitan","CAM","SAM","JSA","Karanjawala","Parekh & Co."],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:223,q:"What is a hash value and why does it matter in electronic evidence under the BSA?",a:"A hash value (SHA1, SHA256, or MD5) is a fixed-length alphanumeric string generated by running the electronic record through a cryptographic algorithm -- it functions as a digital fingerprint of the file. If even a single bit of the file changes, the hash value changes completely. In electronic evidence, the hash value in the Section 63 certificate establishes integrity -- that the record tendered in court is identical to the original. At cross-examination: (1) obtain the hash of the file as it exists in the device; (2) compare with the hash in the certificate; (3) if they differ, the record has been tampered with or corrupted. A certificate that omits the hash value is incomplete and should be objected to on the ground that integrity cannot be verified.",topic:"BSA",companies:["Khaitan","CAM","SAM","JSA","Karanjawala"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:224,q:"Explain the dying declaration provisions under Section 26(a) BSA. Is expectation of death required?",a:"Section 26(a) BSA (old Section 32(1) IEA) makes relevant the statement of a person who is dead or cannot be found, if made as to the cause of death or circumstances of the transaction resulting in death. The expectation of death at the time of making the statement is NOT required in India -- the statement is admissible regardless of whether the maker expected to die. The Supreme Court has held that a dying declaration can be the sole basis of conviction if it is voluntary, truthful, and free from suspicion. Practical safeguards courts look for: fitness certificate from a doctor, recording by a Magistrate, endorsement that the declarant was in a fit state of mind. If the victim survives, the statement is no longer a dying declaration but may be relevant as a prior statement under other provisions.",topic:"BSA",companies:["Khaitan","CAM","SAM","Karanjawala"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:225,q:"Map the key old IEA section numbers to their new BSA equivalents.",a:"Critical renumbering: Old Section 27 (discovery by accused in police custody) = BSA Section 23(2) proviso. Old Section 32(1) (dying declaration) = BSA Section 26(a). Old Section 65B (electronic records certificate) = BSA Section 63. Old Section 90 (30-year-old document presumption) = BSA Section 92. Old Section 114 (court may presume -- illustrations) = BSA Section 119. Old Section 145 (cross-examination on prior written statement) = BSA Section 148. Old Section 154 (hostile witness) = BSA Section 157. Old Section 165 (judge's questions) = BSA Section 168. The five Explanations for electronic primary evidence (Explanations 4-7 to Section 57) are new -- no IEA equivalent. Dual-signature certificate and hash value requirement are also new.",topic:"BSA",companies:["Khaitan","CAM","SAM","JSA","Karanjawala","Agarwal Law Associates","Parekh & Co."],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:226,q:"Explain Section 157 BSA (hostile witnesses -- old Section 154 IEA).",a:"Section 157 BSA allows a party who calls a witness to apply for permission to put questions in cross-examination if the witness gives hostile or adverse evidence. With leave, the party can cross-examine their own witness on prior statements and credibility. Section 157(2) provides that even after a witness is declared hostile, the court can still rely on the portions of their testimony that are consistent and credible -- the entire testimony is not discarded. Use their prior Section 180 BNSS statement to contradict under Section 148; the prior inconsistent statement becomes relevant under Section 158. The court must still corroborate the hostile witness's testimony with other evidence before relying on it -- a conviction cannot ordinarily rest solely on hostile portions.",topic:"BSA",companies:["Khaitan","CAM","SAM","Karanjawala","JSA"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:227,q:"Explain advocate-client privilege under Section 132 BSA. Does it extend to in-house counsel?",a:"Section 132 BSA prohibits an advocate from disclosing: (1) communications from a client during the professional engagement; (2) advice given; (3) contents of documents obtained while acting as advocate. Exceptions: (1) crime or fraud -- if the client sought advice to facilitate crime or fraud, privilege does not apply; (2) if the client voluntarily gives evidence on the same subject (Section 134 BSA). Privilege extends to interpreters, clerks, and employees of the advocate (Section 132(3)). In-house counsel: the BSA uses the term 'advocate' (enrolled under the Advocates Act) -- in-house lawyers who are enrolled advocates arguably attract the privilege, but this is contested since they are employees, not independent legal advisers.",topic:"BSA",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","NDA"],track:"Both",detailed:true},
{id:228,q:"Explain the parol evidence rule under Sections 94-95 BSA and its exceptions.",a:"Section 94 BSA provides that when terms of a contract, grant, or other disposition are reduced to writing, no evidence shall be given to contradict, vary, add to, or subtract from it. The six provisos (exceptions) to Section 95 are: (1) fraud, misrepresentation, or illegality vitiating the document; (2) a separate oral agreement on a matter on which the document is silent (if consistent); (3) a condition precedent to the document's operation; (4) subsequent oral variation or rescission; (5) usage or custom of trade; (6) language to facts -- oral evidence to explain ambiguous reference to external facts. Registered sale deed reciting full payment: seller cannot contradict by oral evidence (rule applies) but can plead fraud (exception 1 to Section 95).",topic:"BSA",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Both",detailed:true},
{id:229,q:"Your opponent tenders Google Maps location history without any Section 63 certificate. List every objection.",a:"Objections in order: (1) No certificate: Section 63 BSA makes a certificate mandatory for secondary electronic evidence -- objection must be taken at the stage of tendering, not later (Section 169 -- harmless error may not apply if objection is not timely). (2) No proof of device ownership or lawful control -- Section 63(2)(a) requires proof that the person responsible for the device had lawful control. (3) No hash value -- the certificate omits the integrity check; the record could have been altered. (4) Authorship gap -- Section 93 BSA does not presume the identity of the sender; location history showing a phone's location does not prove who possessed the phone. (5) Privacy objection -- location data may require court to assess legality of collection. If you sit silent until appeal, Section 169 may render the error harmless -- so take the objection at the earliest stage and ensure it is recorded.",topic:"BSA",companies:["General","Karanjawala","Parekh & Co."],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:230,q:"Explain presumptions for electronic records under the BSA -- Sections 81, 85, 86, 87, 90, 93.",a:"Key presumptions: Section 81 -- e-Gazette publications are presumed genuine. Section 85 -- electronic contracts bearing digital signatures of parties are presumed to have been entered into in the manner stated. Section 86 -- secure electronic records and secure electronic signatures are presumed authentic. Section 87 -- Electronic Signature Certificates are presumed genuine. Section 90 (old Section 90 IEA) -- electronic records five years old produced from proper custody raise a presumption of authenticity (new -- the old IEA only applied to paper documents). Section 93 -- NO presumption exists as to the sender of an electronic message; only presumes the message was dispatched as stated. Critical gap: a party relying on email contracts must still prove who sent the email -- Section 90 does not solve the authorship problem.",topic:"BSA",companies:["Khaitan","CAM","SAM","JSA","Karanjawala"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:231,q:"Section 148 BSA -- cross-examination on prior written statements. Explain the exact mechanics.",a:"Section 148 BSA (old Section 145 IEA) governs cross-examination on prior written statements. The mechanics: (1) You need not show the writing to the witness before questioning on it, but you must ask them about the relevant part before contradicting with it; (2) if you intend to contradict the witness, you must draw their attention to those parts of the writing which are to be used for contradiction; (3) after confronting the witness, the writing must be 'proved' -- put in evidence. For a prior deposition, the mechanics are: 'You gave evidence on [date] in [case]. I am showing you pages [X-Y]. You told the court [specific statement]. Do you maintain that today?' -- if denied, tender the deposition as a document. The prior inconsistent statement becomes admissible under Section 158(c) as going to credit.",topic:"BSA",companies:["CAM","SAM","Khaitan","Karanjawala","JSA"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:232,q:"How do you prove an email chain, WhatsApp conversation, and CCTV footage in a civil trial under the BSA?",a:"Email chain: obtain a Section 63 certificate from the email server provider or your own mail server administrator; the certificate must cover device particulars, hash values, and lawful control. The sender's identity must be proved separately -- Section 93 does not presume authorship. WhatsApp conversation: export the chat as a file; the Section 63 certificate must be signed by the device owner/person in charge; hash the exported file. If the conversation is on the opponent's device, a certificate from the opponent or a court-appointed forensic examiner is needed. CCTV footage: chain of custody from seizure; the recording must be hashed at seizure (panchnama); the DVR/server administrator signs the certificate; a technical expert may be needed to authenticate proper functioning of the recording equipment (Section 63(2) conditions). In all cases: tender the certificate with the document; ensure the person who signed is available for cross-examination.",topic:"BSA",companies:["Khaitan","CAM","SAM","JSA","Karanjawala","Parekh & Co."],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:233,q:"Explain Section 119 BSA (court may presume -- illustrations) and illustration (g) on adverse inference.",a:"Section 119 BSA lists situations where the court 'may presume' a fact (discretionary). Key illustrations: (a) recent unexplained possession of stolen goods -- court may presume the possessor committed the theft; (g) evidence withheld -- when a party in a position to produce evidence fails to do so, the court may presume the evidence would have been unfavourable. Illustration (g) is the most powerful tool in civil litigation: if the opposing party suppresses documents despite being in control of them, invoke Section 119 illustration (g) in final arguments. Preconditions: the party must have been in a position to produce the document; they must have failed without adequate explanation; and the document would have been material to the issues. This cannot reverse the burden of proof entirely but supplements inference from other evidence.",topic:"BSA",companies:["CAM","SAM","Khaitan","Karanjawala","JSA"],track:"Litigation",detailed:true},
{id:234,q:"Explain Section 121 BSA -- estoppel: ingredients and distinction from waiver and res judicata.",a:"Section 121 BSA (old Section 115 IEA): when one person by declaration, act, or omission intentionally caused another to believe a thing to be true and to act upon such belief, neither he nor his representative shall be allowed to deny the truth of that thing. Three ingredients: (1) a representation by the party to be estopped; (2) reliance on that representation; (3) change of position (detriment) by the relying party. Estoppel vs. waiver: waiver is voluntary surrender of a known right, does not require reliance. Estoppel vs. res judicata: res judicata bars the suit/issue; estoppel bars a particular representation being denied; estoppel is a rule of evidence (operating on facts), res judicata is a rule of public policy (operating on judgments). Estoppel cannot override a statute. A minor's plea of minority cannot be estopped. Estoppel must be specifically pleaded.",topic:"BSA",companies:["CAM","SAM","AZB","Khaitan","Trilegal","JSA"],track:"Both",detailed:true},
];

const CS={navy:"#0f172a",navyL:"#1e293b",navyM:"#334155",ox:"#7c2d3a",oxS:"rgba(124,45,58,0.08)",brass:"#b8860b",brassL:"#d4a843",brassS:"rgba(184,134,11,0.12)",cream:"#faf7f2",creamD:"#f1ebe0",creamDD:"#e5ddd0",slate:"#64748b",text:"#1e293b",textL:"#475569",textLL:"#94a3b8",border:"#e2d9cc",white:"#ffffff",green:"#15803d",red:"#b91c1c"};
const font={display:"Georgia,'Palatino Linotype',serif",body:"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Segoe UI',system-ui,sans-serif",mono:"'SF Mono',Menlo,Consolas,monospace"};

function CompanyTag({name}){
  const isGen=name==="General";
  return <span style={{fontSize:11,fontWeight:600,padding:"2px 8px",borderRadius:6,background:isGen?"rgba(100,116,139,0.08)":CS.brassS,color:isGen?CS.slate:CS.brass,border:"1px solid "+(isGen?"transparent":"rgba(184,134,11,0.2)"),fontFamily:font.body}}>{name}</span>;
}

function Nav({active,setActive}){
  return (
    <nav style={{position:"sticky",top:0,zIndex:100,background:CS.navy,borderBottom:"1px solid "+CS.navyM,display:"flex",alignItems:"center",justifyContent:"space-between",padding:"0 24px",height:56,fontFamily:font.body}}>
      <div style={{display:"flex",alignItems:"center",gap:8,cursor:"pointer"}} onClick={()=>setActive("home")}>
        <span style={{color:CS.brass,fontSize:22,fontFamily:font.display,fontWeight:700}}>§</span>
        <span style={{color:CS.cream,fontSize:15,fontWeight:700,letterSpacing:"0.04em"}}>LegallySo<span style={{color:CS.brassL}}>und</span></span>
      </div>
      <div style={{display:"flex",gap:4,alignItems:"center"}}>
        {[{id:"home",label:"Home"},{id:"qa",label:"Questions"},{id:"sim",label:"Live Interview Prep"},{id:"community",label:"Community"}].map(it=>(
          <button key={it.id} onClick={()=>setActive(it.id)} style={{background:active===it.id?"rgba(184,134,11,0.15)":"transparent",color:active===it.id?CS.brassL:CS.textLL,border:"none",padding:"8px 12px",borderRadius:8,cursor:"pointer",fontSize:12,fontWeight:600,fontFamily:font.body}}>{it.label}</button>
        ))}
      </div>
    </nav>
  );
}

function Hero({setActive}){
  return (
    <section style={{background:"linear-gradient(135deg,"+CS.navy+" 0%,"+CS.navyL+" 100%)",padding:"72px 24px 64px",textAlign:"center"}}>
      <div style={{maxWidth:700,margin:"0 auto"}}>
        <div style={{color:CS.brass,fontSize:12,fontWeight:700,letterSpacing:"0.15em",marginBottom:16,fontFamily:font.body}}>INDIA LAW INTERVIEW PREPARATION</div>
        <h1 style={{color:CS.cream,fontSize:"clamp(28px,5vw,48px)",fontFamily:font.display,fontWeight:700,lineHeight:1.15,margin:"0 0 20px"}}>LegallySo<span style={{color:CS.brassL}}>und</span></h1>
        <p style={{color:CS.textLL,fontSize:16,lineHeight:1.7,margin:"0 auto 16px",fontFamily:font.body,maxWidth:580}}>Your complete preparation toolkit for law firm interviews in India. Real questions from Tier-1 firm interviews with detailed answers, firm tags, topic filters, and an AI-powered mock interview simulator.</p>
        <p style={{color:"rgba(148,163,184,0.7)",fontSize:13,lineHeight:1.6,margin:"0 auto 32px",fontFamily:font.body,maxWidth:560}}>Covering Corporate M&amp;A, PE/VC, Banking &amp; Finance, Capital Markets, Disputes, Arbitration, IBC, Constitutional Law, and more.</p>
        <div style={{display:"flex",gap:12,justifyContent:"center",flexWrap:"wrap"}}>
          <button onClick={()=>setActive("qa")} style={{background:CS.brass,color:CS.navy,border:"none",padding:"12px 28px",borderRadius:8,fontSize:14,fontWeight:700,cursor:"pointer",fontFamily:font.body}}>Browse Questions</button>
          <button onClick={()=>setActive("sim")} style={{background:"transparent",color:CS.cream,border:"1px solid rgba(250,247,242,0.3)",padding:"12px 28px",borderRadius:8,fontSize:14,fontWeight:600,cursor:"pointer",fontFamily:font.body}}>Try Live Simulator</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  );
}

function HomeOverview({setActive}){
  const cards=[
    {icon:"📖",title:"Question Bank",desc:"80+ conceptual, statutory, and scenario questions indexed by Tier-1 Indian law firm tags with explicit technical filters.",action:"Start reading",id:"qa"},
    {icon:"🤖",title:"Partner Simulator",desc:"Simulate an interview with a top law firm partner. Powered by Qwen AI to deliver custom questions, legal counters, and final analytics reports.",action:"Launch simulator",id:"sim"},
    {icon:"🏛",title:"Aspirant Directory",desc:"Network with fellow students, clerks, and associates preparing for corporate tracks and litigation chambers across major Indian cities.",action:"Open Directory",id:"community"},
  ];
  return (
    <div style={{maxWidth:1000,margin:"48px auto",padding:"0 24px"}}>
      <div style={{display:"grid",gridTemplateColumns:"repeat(auto-fit,minmax(280px,1fr))",gap:24}}>
        {cards.map(c=>(
          <div key={c.id} style={{background:CS.white,border:"1px solid "+CS.border,borderRadius:12,padding:28,display:"flex",flexDirection:"column"}}>
            <div style={{fontSize:24,marginBottom:12}}>{c.icon}</div>
            <h2 style={{fontSize:18,fontFamily:font.display,color:CS.text,margin:"0 0 10px"}}>{c.title}</h2>
            <p style={{color:CS.textL,fontSize:14,lineHeight:1.6,margin:0,fontFamily:font.body,flex:1}}>{c.desc}</p>
            <button onClick={()=>setActive(c.id)} style={{background:"transparent",border:"none",color:CS.brass,fontWeight:700,fontSize:13,cursor:"pointer",padding:0,marginTop:20,textAlign:"left",fontFamily:font.body}}>{c.action} →</button>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}

function QuestionBank(){
  const [selTopic,setSelTopic]=useState("All");
  const [selComp,setSelComp]=useState("All");
  const [selTrack,setSelTrack]=useState("All");
  const [search,setSearch]=useState("");
  const [exp,setExp]=useState({});
  const toggle=(id)=>setExp(p=>({...p,[id]:!p[id]}));
  const filtered=QS.filter(q=>{
    if(selTopic!=="All"&&q.topic!==selTopic)return false;
    if(selComp!=="All"&&!q.companies.includes(selComp))return false;
    if(selTrack!=="All"&&q.track!==selTrack&&q.track!=="Both")return false;
    if(search.trim()){const s=search.toLowerCase();return q.q.toLowerCase().includes(s)||q.a.toLowerCase().includes(s);}
    return true;
  });
  const ss={padding:"10px 14px",borderRadius:8,border:"1px solid "+CS.border,fontSize:13,fontFamily:font.body,color:CS.text,background:CS.white};
  return (
    <div style={{maxWidth:900,margin:"40px auto",padding:"0 24px"}}>
      <h2 style={{fontSize:22,fontFamily:font.display,color:CS.text,margin:"0 0 24px"}}>Tier-1 Interview Questions Database</h2>
      <div className="filter-bar" style={{background:CS.white,border:"1px solid "+CS.border,borderRadius:12,padding:20,marginBottom:28,display:"flex",gap:12,flexWrap:"wrap",alignItems:"center"}}>
        <input type="text" placeholder="Search concept, clause, or judgment..." value={search} onChange={e=>setSearch(e.target.value)} style={{...ss,flex:1,minWidth:200,background:CS.creamD}}/>
        <select value={selTopic} onChange={e=>setSelTopic(e.target.value)} style={ss}><option value="All">All Topics</option>{TOPICS.map(t=><option key={t} value={t}>{t}</option>)}</select>
        <select value={selComp} onChange={e=>setSelComp(e.target.value)} style={ss}><option value="All">All Firms</option>{COMPANIES.map(c=><option key={c} value={c}>{c}</option>)}</select>
        <select value={selTrack} onChange={e=>setSelTrack(e.target.value)} style={ss}>{TRACKS.map(t=><option key={t} value={t}>{t} Track</option>)}</select>
      </div>
      <p style={{color:CS.textL,fontSize:13,fontFamily:font.body,margin:"0 0 16px"}}>Showing <strong>{filtered.length}</strong> of {QS.length} questions</p>
      <div style={{display:"flex",flexDirection:"column",gap:12}}>
        {filtered.length===0?(
          <div style={{background:CS.white,border:"1px solid "+CS.border,borderRadius:12,padding:32,textAlign:"center",color:CS.textL,fontFamily:font.body}}>No questions match your filters. Try broadening your search.</div>
        ):filtered.map(q=>(
          <div key={q.id} style={{background:CS.white,border:"1px solid "+(exp[q.id]?CS.brass:CS.border),borderRadius:12,padding:22,cursor:"pointer",transition:"border-color 0.2s",boxShadow:exp[q.id]?"0 2px 12px rgba(184,134,11,0.1)":"none"}} onClick={()=>toggle(q.id)}>
            <div style={{display:"flex",justifyContent:"space-between",alignItems:"flex-start",gap:12}}>
              <div style={{flex:1}}>
                <div style={{display:"flex",gap:6,flexWrap:"wrap",marginBottom:8,alignItems:"center"}}>
                  <span style={{color:CS.brass,fontFamily:font.display,fontWeight:700,fontSize:12}}>{q.topic}</span>
                  {q.detailed&&<span style={{fontSize:10,background:CS.oxS,color:CS.ox,padding:"2px 6px",borderRadius:4,fontWeight:700,fontFamily:font.body}}>DETAILED</span>}
                  {q.companies.map(c=><CompanyTag key={c} name={c}/>)}
                </div>
                <h3 style={{fontSize:15,fontFamily:font.body,fontWeight:600,color:CS.text,lineHeight:1.4,margin:0}}>{q.q}</h3>
              </div>
              <span style={{fontSize:20,color:exp[q.id]?CS.brass:CS.textLL,transform:exp[q.id]?"rotate(45deg)":"none",transition:"transform 0.2s",flexShrink:0,marginTop:2}}>+</span>
            </div>
            {exp[q.id]&&(
              <div style={{marginTop:16,paddingTop:16,borderTop:"1px dashed "+CS.border,color:CS.textL,fontSize:14,lineHeight:1.7,fontFamily:font.body}}>{q.a}</div>
            )}
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}

function Simulator(){
  const [phase,setPhase]=useState("setup");
  const [backendStatus,setBackendStatus]=useState("checking");
  const [backendMsg,setBackendMsg]=useState("");
  const [canUse,setCanUse]=useState(null);
  const [interviewType,setInterviewType]=useState("Corporate M&A");
  const [difficulty,setDifficulty]=useState("Intermediate");
  const [messages,setMessages]=useState([]);
  const [input,setInput]=useState("");
  const [loading,setLoading]=useState(false);
  const [timeLeft,setTimeLeft]=useState(15*60);
  const [evaluation,setEvaluation]=useState(null);
  const [error,setError]=useState("");
  const timerRef=useRef(null);
  const chatRef=useRef(null);
  const types=["Corporate M&A","PE / VC Investment","Banking & Finance","Capital Markets / SEBI","Arbitration & Disputes","IBC / Insolvency","Criminal / White Collar","Constitutional & Writ Practice","General Corporate","Competition Law","Litigation Chamber","CPC & Civil Procedure","BSA & Evidence Law"];

  useEffect(()=>{
    (async()=>{
      try{
        const r=await fetch(BACKEND_URL+"/api/health",{signal:AbortSignal.timeout(5000)});
        const d=await r.json();
        if(d.ok&&d.keyLoaded){setBackendStatus("ok");setBackendMsg("Connected — Qwen ("+d.model+") ready");}
        else if(d.ok&&!d.keyLoaded){setBackendStatus("error");setBackendMsg("Backend running but OPENROUTER_KEY not set. Edit .env and restart.");}
        else{setBackendStatus("error");setBackendMsg("Backend responded unexpectedly.");}
      }catch(e){
        setBackendStatus("error");
        setBackendMsg("Cannot reach backend at "+BACKEND_URL+". Start the server: cd legallysound-backend && node server.js");
      }
    })();
  },[]);

  useEffect(()=>{
    (async()=>{try{const today=new Date().toISOString().split("T")[0];const res=await window.storage.get("sim-last-used");setCanUse(!res||res.value!==today);}catch(e){setCanUse(true);}})();
  },[]);

  useEffect(()=>{
    if(phase==="active"&&timeLeft>0){
      timerRef.current=setInterval(()=>setTimeLeft(t=>{if(t<=1){clearInterval(timerRef.current);return 0;}return t-1;}),1000);
      return()=>clearInterval(timerRef.current);
    }
    if(timeLeft===0&&phase==="active"){endInterview();}
  },[phase,timeLeft]);

  useEffect(()=>{if(chatRef.current)chatRef.current.scrollTop=chatRef.current.scrollHeight;},[messages,loading]);

  const fmtTime=s=>Math.floor(s/60)+":"+(String(s%60).padStart(2,"0"));

  const sysPrompt="You are a senior legal interviewer at a top-tier Indian law firm conducting a "+interviewType+" interview at "+difficulty+" difficulty level. Ask ONE question at a time. Wait for the candidate's answer before asking the next. Start with an introductory question, then probe deeper. After each response, briefly acknowledge in 1 line then ask the next question. Ask follow-ups based on answers to test depth. Keep questions relevant to "+interviewType+" practice in India. Be professional but probing like a real Tier-1 firm partner. Do NOT list multiple questions at once. Vary types: conceptual, statutory, case-law, scenario-based. Gently correct wrong answers and move on.";

  const callBackend=async(endpoint,body)=>{
    const r=await fetch(BACKEND_URL+endpoint,{method:"POST",headers:{"Content-Type":"application/json"},body:JSON.stringify(body),signal:AbortSignal.timeout(30000)});
    if(!r.ok){const e=await r.json().catch(()=>({}));throw new Error(e.error||"Server error "+r.status);}
    return r.json();
  };

  const startInterview=async()=>{
    if(backendStatus!=="ok"){setError("Backend not ready. "+backendMsg);return;}
    setError("");setLoading(true);
    try{
      const d=await callBackend("/api/chat",{systemPrompt:sysPrompt,messages:[{role:"user",content:"Begin the "+interviewType+" interview. Greet the candidate and ask your first question."}]});
      setMessages([{role:"assistant",content:d.reply}]);setPhase("active");setTimeLeft(15*60);
    }catch(e){setError("Failed to start: "+e.message);}
    setLoading(false);
  };

  const sendMessage=async()=>{
    if(!input.trim()||loading)return;
    const u={role:"user",content:input.trim()};const n=[...messages,u];setMessages(n);setInput("");setLoading(true);setError("");
    try{const d=await callBackend("/api/chat",{systemPrompt:sysPrompt,messages:n});setMessages([...n,{role:"assistant",content:d.reply}]);}
    catch(e){setError("API error: "+e.message);}
    setLoading(false);
  };

  const endInterview=async()=>{
    clearInterval(timerRef.current);setPhase("evaluating");
    const transcript=messages.map(m=>(m.role==="user"?"CANDIDATE":"INTERVIEWER")+": "+m.content).join("\n\n");
    const evalPrompt="You are a senior HR evaluator at a top-tier Indian law firm. Review this "+interviewType+" interview transcript and provide a structured evaluation.\n\nTRANSCRIPT:\n"+transcript+"\n\nProvide evaluation in EXACTLY this format:\nLEGAL KNOWLEDGE: [score]/10\n[1-2 line comment]\n\nCOMMUNICATION: [score]/10\n[1-2 line comment]\n\nPROBING DEPTH RESPONSE: [score]/10\n[1-2 line comment]\n\nVERDICT: [Hired / Deferred / Rejected]\n\nFINAL ADVICE: [3-4 lines of constructive advice]";
    try{
      const d=await callBackend("/api/evaluate",{evaluationPrompt:evalPrompt});
      setEvaluation(d.result);
      try{await window.storage.set("sim-last-used",new Date().toISOString().split("T")[0]);setCanUse(false);}catch(e){}
    }catch(e){setEvaluation("Evaluation could not be generated: "+e.message);}
    setPhase("result");
  };

  if(canUse===null)return <div style={{padding:48,textAlign:"center",color:CS.textL,fontFamily:font.body}}>Loading...</div>;

  return (
    <div style={{maxWidth:800,margin:"40px auto",padding:"0 24px"}}>
      <h2 style={{fontSize:22,fontFamily:font.display,color:CS.text,margin:"0 0 6px"}}>AI Mock Partner Interview</h2>
      <p style={{color:CS.textL,fontSize:14,fontFamily:font.body,margin:"0 0 20px"}}>One 15-minute session per day. API key stored securely on the backend server.</p>

      <div style={{display:"flex",alignItems:"center",gap:10,padding:"10px 16px",borderRadius:8,marginBottom:24,background:backendStatus==="ok"?"rgba(21,128,61,0.08)":backendStatus==="error"?"rgba(185,28,28,0.08)":"rgba(184,134,11,0.08)",border:"1px solid "+(backendStatus==="ok"?"rgba(21,128,61,0.3)":backendStatus==="error"?"rgba(185,28,28,0.3)":"rgba(184,134,11,0.3)")}}>
        <span style={{fontSize:14,flexShrink:0}}>{backendStatus==="ok"?"✓":backendStatus==="error"?"✗":"⋯"}</span>
        <div>
          <p style={{margin:0,fontSize:13,fontWeight:600,color:CS.text,fontFamily:font.body}}>{backendStatus==="ok"?"Backend Connected":backendStatus==="error"?"Backend Unavailable":"Checking backend..."}</p>
          {backendMsg&&<p style={{margin:"2px 0 0",fontSize:12,color:CS.textL,fontFamily:font.body}}>{backendMsg}</p>}
        </div>
      </div>

      {phase==="setup"&&(
        <div>
          {!canUse&&<div style={{background:CS.brassS,border:"1px solid "+CS.brass,borderRadius:10,padding:20,marginBottom:24,textAlign:"center"}}><p style={{margin:0,fontSize:14,fontWeight:600,color:CS.text,fontFamily:font.body}}>You have used your session for today.</p><p style={{margin:"8px 0 0",fontSize:13,color:CS.textL,fontFamily:font.body}}>Come back tomorrow for another mock interview.</p></div>}
          <div style={{background:CS.white,border:"1px solid "+CS.border,borderRadius:12,padding:28}}>
            <div style={{marginBottom:20}}>
              <label style={{display:"block",fontSize:12,fontWeight:700,color:CS.textL,marginBottom:6,fontFamily:font.body}}>INTERVIEW TYPE</label>
              <select value={interviewType} onChange={e=>setInterviewType(e.target.value)} style={{width:"100%",padding:"10px 14px",borderRadius:8,border:"1px solid "+CS.border,fontSize:13,fontFamily:font.body,color:CS.text,background:CS.white}}>{types.map(t=><option key={t} value={t}>{t}</option>)}</select>
            </div>
            <div style={{marginBottom:24}}>
              <label style={{display:"block",fontSize:12,fontWeight:700,color:CS.textL,marginBottom:6,fontFamily:font.body}}>DIFFICULTY</label>
              <div style={{display:"flex",gap:8}}>
                {["Beginner","Intermediate","Advanced"].map(d=><button key={d} onClick={()=>setDifficulty(d)} style={{flex:1,padding:"10px 0",borderRadius:8,fontSize:13,fontWeight:600,fontFamily:font.body,cursor:"pointer",background:difficulty===d?CS.navy:"transparent",color:difficulty===d?CS.cream:CS.text,border:"1.5px solid "+(difficulty===d?CS.navy:CS.border)}}>{d}</button>)}
              </div>
            </div>
            {error&&<p style={{color:CS.red,fontSize:13,fontFamily:font.body,margin:"0 0 16px"}}>{error}</p>}
            <button onClick={startInterview} disabled={!canUse||loading||backendStatus!=="ok"} style={{width:"100%",padding:"14px 0",borderRadius:8,border:"none",background:(canUse&&backendStatus==="ok")?CS.brass:CS.creamDD,color:(canUse&&backendStatus==="ok")?CS.navy:CS.textLL,fontSize:14,fontWeight:700,cursor:(canUse&&backendStatus==="ok")?"pointer":"not-allowed",fontFamily:font.body}}>{loading?"Connecting...":backendStatus!=="ok"?"Backend Unavailable":"Start Interview"}</button>
          </div>
        </div>
      )}

      {phase==="active"&&(
        <div style={{display:"flex",flexDirection:"column",height:"min(70vh,600px)"}}>
          <div style={{display:"flex",justifyContent:"space-between",alignItems:"center",padding:"12px 16px",background:CS.cream,borderRadius:"10px 10px 0 0",border:"1px solid "+CS.border,borderBottom:"none"}}>
            <div style={{display:"flex",alignItems:"center",gap:12}}>
              <span style={{fontSize:12,fontWeight:600,fontFamily:font.body,color:CS.textL}}>{interviewType}</span>
              <span style={{fontSize:11,padding:"2px 8px",borderRadius:8,fontWeight:600,fontFamily:font.body,background:difficulty==="Advanced"?CS.oxS:difficulty==="Intermediate"?CS.brassS:"rgba(21,128,61,0.08)",color:difficulty==="Advanced"?CS.ox:difficulty==="Intermediate"?CS.brass:CS.green}}>{difficulty}</span>
            </div>
            <div style={{display:"flex",alignItems:"center",gap:12}}>
              <span style={{fontSize:16,fontWeight:700,fontFamily:font.mono,color:timeLeft<120?CS.red:timeLeft<300?CS.brass:CS.text}}>{fmtTime(timeLeft)}</span>
              <button onClick={endInterview} style={{fontSize:12,fontWeight:600,padding:"6px 14px",borderRadius:6,border:"1px solid "+CS.ox,background:"transparent",color:CS.ox,cursor:"pointer",fontFamily:font.body}}>End &amp; Evaluate</button>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div ref={chatRef} style={{flex:1,overflow:"auto",padding:20,background:CS.white,border:"1px solid "+CS.border,display:"flex",flexDirection:"column",gap:16}}>
            {messages.map((m,i)=>(
              <div key={i} style={{alignSelf:m.role==="user"?"flex-end":"flex-start",maxWidth:"80%"}}>
                <div style={{fontSize:10,fontWeight:700,color:m.role==="user"?CS.slate:CS.brass,marginBottom:4,fontFamily:font.body,textAlign:m.role==="user"?"right":"left",letterSpacing:"0.05em"}}>{m.role==="user"?"YOU":"PARTNER INTERVIEWER"}</div>
                <div style={{padding:"12px 16px",borderRadius:12,fontSize:14,lineHeight:1.5,fontFamily:font.body,background:m.role==="user"?CS.creamD:CS.cream,color:CS.text,border:"1px solid "+CS.border,whiteSpace:"pre-wrap"}}>{m.content}</div>
              </div>
            ))}
            {loading&&<div style={{color:CS.slate,fontSize:13,fontFamily:font.body,fontStyle:"italic",alignSelf:"flex-start"}}>Partner is thinking...</div>}
            {error&&<div style={{color:CS.red,fontSize:13,fontFamily:font.body}}>{error}</div>}
          </div>
          <div style={{display:"flex",gap:8,paddingTop:12}}>
            <input type="text" placeholder="Type your answer..." value={input} onChange={e=>setInput(e.target.value)} onKeyDown={e=>e.key==="Enter"&&sendMessage()} disabled={loading} style={{flex:1,padding:"12px 16px",borderRadius:8,border:"1px solid "+CS.border,fontSize:14,fontFamily:font.body}}/>
            <button onClick={sendMessage} disabled={loading||!input.trim()} style={{padding:"0 24px",borderRadius:8,border:"none",background:CS.navy,color:CS.cream,fontWeight:600,fontSize:13,cursor:"pointer",fontFamily:font.body,opacity:loading?0.5:1}}>Send</button>
          </div>
        </div>
      )}

      {phase==="evaluating"&&<div style={{background:CS.white,border:"1px solid "+CS.border,borderRadius:12,padding:40,textAlign:"center",fontFamily:font.body}}><p style={{fontSize:16,fontWeight:600,color:CS.text,margin:0}}>Evaluating your performance...</p><p style={{color:CS.slate,fontSize:13,margin:"6px 0 0"}}>The HR evaluator is reviewing your transcript.</p></div>}

      {phase==="result"&&(
        <div style={{background:CS.white,border:"1px solid "+CS.border,borderRadius:12,padding:32}}>
          <h3 style={{fontSize:18,fontFamily:font.display,color:CS.text,margin:"0 0 20px"}}>Interview Evaluation</h3>
          <div style={{whiteSpace:"pre-wrap",fontFamily:font.mono,fontSize:13,background:CS.cream,padding:20,borderRadius:8,border:"1px solid "+CS.border,lineHeight:1.6,color:CS.navy}}>{evaluation||"Evaluation could not be generated."}</div>
          <button onClick={()=>{setPhase("setup");setMessages([]);setEvaluation(null);setTimeLeft(15*60);}} style={{marginTop:24,padding:"10px 20px",background:CS.navy,color:CS.cream,border:"none",borderRadius:6,fontSize:13,fontWeight:600,cursor:"pointer",fontFamily:font.body}}>Start New Session</button>
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}

function Community(){
  const [members,setMembers]=useState([]);
  const [name,setName]=useState("");
  const [track,setTrack]=useState("Corporate");
  const [desc,setDesc]=useState("");
  const [linkedin,setLinkedin]=useState("");
  const [agreed,setAgreed]=useState(false);
  const [loading,setLoading]=useState(true);
  const [submitting,setSubmitting]=useState(false);
  const [success,setSuccess]=useState(false);

  useEffect(()=>{
    (async()=>{try{const r=await window.storage.get("com-members-v2",true);if(r&&r.value)setMembers(JSON.parse(r.value));}catch(e){}setLoading(false);})();
  },[]);

  const handleJoin=async()=>{
    if(!name.trim()||!desc.trim()||!agreed)return;setSubmitting(true);
    const item={name:name.trim(),track,desc:desc.trim(),linkedin:linkedin.trim(),joined:new Date().toISOString().split("T")[0]};
    const updated=[...members,item];
    try{await window.storage.set("com-members-v2",JSON.stringify(updated),true);setMembers(updated);setSuccess(true);setName("");setDesc("");setLinkedin("");setAgreed(false);setTimeout(()=>setSuccess(false),4000);}catch(e){}
    setSubmitting(false);
  };

  return (
    <div style={{maxWidth:1000,margin:"40px auto",padding:"0 24px"}}>
      <h2 style={{fontSize:22,fontFamily:font.display,color:CS.text,margin:"0 0 6px"}}>Aspirants Directory</h2>
      <p style={{color:CS.textL,fontSize:14,fontFamily:font.body,margin:"0 0 32px"}}>Network with fellow students, clerks, and associates preparing for placements.</p>
      <div className="community-grid" style={{display:"grid",gridTemplateColumns:"340px 1fr",gap:32,alignItems:"start"}}>
        <div style={{background:CS.white,border:"1px solid "+CS.border,borderRadius:12,padding:24}}>
          <h3 style={{fontSize:16,fontFamily:font.display,color:CS.text,margin:"0 0 16px"}}>Register Your Profile</h3>
          <div style={{marginBottom:14}}>
            <label style={{display:"block",fontSize:11,fontWeight:700,color:CS.textL,marginBottom:4,fontFamily:font.body}}>NAME</label>
            <input type="text" value={name} onChange={e=>setName(e.target.value)} placeholder="e.g. Rohan Sharma" style={{width:"100%",padding:"8px 12px",borderRadius:6,border:"1px solid "+CS.border,fontSize:13,fontFamily:font.body,boxSizing:"border-box"}}/>
          </div>
          <div style={{marginBottom:14}}>
            <label style={{display:"block",fontSize:11,fontWeight:700,color:CS.textL,marginBottom:4,fontFamily:font.body}}>PRACTICE PREFERENCE</label>
            <select value={track} onChange={e=>setTrack(e.target.value)} style={{width:"100%",padding:"8px 12px",borderRadius:6,border:"1px solid "+CS.border,fontSize:13,fontFamily:font.body,background:CS.white,color:CS.text,boxSizing:"border-box"}}>
              <option value="Corporate">Corporate / Transactional</option>
              <option value="Litigation">Litigation / Dispute Res</option>
              <option value="Both">General / Clerks</option>
            </select>
          </div>
          <div style={{marginBottom:14}}>
            <label style={{display:"block",fontSize:11,fontWeight:700,color:CS.textL,marginBottom:4,fontFamily:font.body}}>ABOUT YOU</label>
            <textarea value={desc} onChange={e=>setDesc(e.target.value)} rows={3} placeholder="e.g. Final year NLS. Interned at CAM, AZB. Prepping for infrastructure groups." style={{width:"100%",padding:"8px 12px",borderRadius:6,border:"1px solid "+CS.border,fontSize:13,fontFamily:font.body,resize:"none",boxSizing:"border-box"}}/>
          </div>
          <div style={{marginBottom:16}}>
            <label style={{display:"block",fontSize:11,fontWeight:700,color:CS.textL,marginBottom:4,fontFamily:font.body}}>LINKEDIN PROFILE <span style={{fontWeight:400,color:CS.textLL}}>(optional)</span></label>
            <input type="text" value={linkedin} onChange={e=>setLinkedin(e.target.value)} placeholder="https://linkedin.com/in/your-profile" style={{width:"100%",padding:"8px 12px",borderRadius:6,border:"1px solid "+CS.border,fontSize:13,fontFamily:font.body,boxSizing:"border-box"}}/>
          </div>
          <label style={{display:"flex",gap:8,alignItems:"flex-start",cursor:"pointer",marginBottom:16}}>
            <input type="checkbox" checked={agreed} onChange={e=>setAgreed(e.target.checked)} style={{marginTop:3}}/>
            <span style={{fontSize:12,color:CS.textL,fontFamily:font.body,lineHeight:1.4}}>I understand my name and description will be visible to all visitors of this page.</span>
          </label>
          {success&&<p style={{color:CS.green,fontSize:13,fontFamily:font.body,margin:"0 0 12px",fontWeight:600}}>Registered successfully!</p>}
          <button onClick={handleJoin} disabled={!name.trim()||!desc.trim()||!agreed||submitting} style={{width:"100%",padding:"12px 0",borderRadius:8,border:"none",background:(name.trim()&&desc.trim()&&agreed)?CS.navy:CS.creamDD,color:(name.trim()&&desc.trim()&&agreed)?CS.cream:CS.textLL,fontSize:13,fontWeight:700,cursor:(name.trim()&&desc.trim()&&agreed)?"pointer":"not-allowed",fontFamily:font.body}}>{submitting?"Registering...":"Join the Community"}</button>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style={{fontSize:16,fontFamily:font.display,color:CS.text,margin:"0 0 16px"}}>Registered Aspirants <span style={{fontSize:13,fontWeight:400,color:CS.textL}}>({members.length})</span></h3>
          {loading?<p style={{color:CS.textL,fontSize:13,fontFamily:font.body}}>Loading directory...</p>:members.length===0?(
            <div style={{background:CS.cream,border:"1px solid "+CS.border,borderRadius:10,padding:24,textAlign:"center"}}><p style={{color:CS.textL,fontSize:13,fontFamily:font.body,margin:0}}>No one has registered yet. Be the first!</p></div>
          ):(
            <div style={{display:"flex",flexDirection:"column",gap:12}}>
              {members.slice().reverse().map((m,i)=>(
                <div key={i} style={{background:CS.white,border:"1px solid "+CS.border,borderRadius:12,padding:20}}>
                  <div style={{display:"flex",justifyContent:"space-between",alignItems:"flex-start",gap:12,marginBottom:8}}>
                    <div style={{display:"flex",alignItems:"center",gap:8}}>
                      <h4 style={{fontSize:15,fontWeight:600,color:CS.text,fontFamily:font.body,margin:0}}>{m.name}</h4>
                      {m.linkedin&&(
                        <a href={m.linkedin.startsWith("http")?m.linkedin:"https://"+m.linkedin} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" title="LinkedIn Profile" style={{color:"#0077B5",display:"flex",alignItems:"center",textDecoration:"none"}}>
                          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor"><path d="M20.447 20.452h-3.554v-5.569c0-1.328-.027-3.037-1.852-3.037-1.853 0-2.136 1.445-2.136 2.939v5.667H9.351V9h3.414v1.561h.046c.477-.9 1.637-1.85 3.37-1.85 3.601 0 4.267 2.37 4.267 5.455v6.286zM5.337 7.433a2.062 2.062 0 01-2.063-2.065 2.064 2.064 0 112.063 2.065zm1.782 13.019H3.555V9h3.564v11.452zM22.225 0H1.771C.792 0 0 .774 0 1.729v20.542C0 23.227.792 24 1.771 24h20.451C23.2 24 24 23.227 24 22.271V1.729C24 .774 23.2 0 22.222 0h.003z"/></svg>
                        </a>
                      )}
                    </div>
                    <div style={{display:"flex",alignItems:"center",gap:8}}>
                      <span style={{fontSize:11,fontWeight:600,padding:"2px 8px",borderRadius:4,background:m.track==="Corporate"?CS.brassS:m.track==="Litigation"?CS.oxS:"rgba(21,128,61,0.08)",color:m.track==="Corporate"?CS.brass:m.track==="Litigation"?CS.ox:CS.green,fontFamily:font.body}}>{m.track}</span>
                      <span style={{fontSize:10,color:CS.textLL,fontFamily:font.body}}>{m.joined}</span>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <p style={{margin:0,fontSize:13,color:CS.textL,lineHeight:1.5,fontFamily:font.body}}>{m.desc}</p>
                </div>
              ))}
            </div>
          )}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}


